MetaWrap2 v=2.1.0
	Phase 0 and the first of Workstream B: the machinery for choosing defaults by measurement,
		the first default it moved, and chimerism detection the marker-gene checks cannot do.
	Changed - the shipped binning tier is metaBAT2 + SemiBin2, not metaBAT2 + MaxBin2
		It is the first configuration measured here that beats metaWRAP 1 on both axes at
			once: end to end on per-sample assemblies, 53 clean high-quality MAGs against
			metaWRAP 1's 48, for 43.8 cpu-hours against 52.5. The previous default returned
			41 for 13.8 and was chosen on a quality-per-cost ratio, which is the mistake the
			tiers exist to fix - ranking on that ratio produced a default that recovers fewer
			genomes than the pipeline it replaces.
		The cost is that a plain run now wants metawrap2-learned, ~1.5 GB of pytorch, so
			quickstart builds it and install-env --binning-only includes it. Anyone who does
			not want it asks for --preset fast and gets exactly the old behaviour.
		One definition, not two: run.DEFAULT_BINNERS is derived from binning.PRESETS, and the
			incumbent marker in benchmarks/configurations.toml moved with it, which a test
			asserts - the code and the results table naming different arms is how a ranker
			ends up comparing challengers against a row nobody runs.
	Fixed - every read_qc figure in the results table was one mate's count
		The scorer wrote one row per mate file. A measurement is keyed on (dataset,
			comparison, variant, sample, metric) and deliberately not on the source file, so
			the two rows collapsed and whichever mate was walked last won. reads_kept,
			bases_kept and q30_bases_kept for the trimmer comparison were therefore half the
			real counts. Both arms were halved identically, so the margin that chose fastp
			holds; the absolute numbers do not.
		Summed at the collection site instead, which is what the dedup key's own docstring
			says has to happen: "a metric that two files contribute to additively is suffixed
			per bin set long before it reaches here" - and read_qc's mates were the one case
			that was not. The test wrote only mate 1 per sample, so it exercised summing
			across samples and never across mates; it now writes both.
	Fixed - a refinement benchmark arm was refined from three of its bin sets, alphabetically
		The runner zipped the *_bins directories it found onto ("-A", "-B", "-C"), and zip
			truncates without complaint. An arm built on four or more binners silently dropped
			the rest - with comebin/concoct/maxbin2/metabat2/semibin2/vamb that drops metaBAT2 -
			so the arm's published numbers came from a different input set than its name.
		Now passed with --bins, which bin_refinement accepts repeatedly, with a leftover
			metawrap_<c>_<x>_bins from an earlier refinement excluded (consolidating a set
			with the sets it came from is not a comparison anyone declared) and more than six
			sets refused rather than quietly subsetted: six is 63 CheckM runs.
	Fixed - a crashed variant's partial output was scored and published as a result
		The runner records a non-zero exit in <comparison>.run.json and discards that
			variant's cost rows. Scoring read that file for data_dir and assembly but not for
			returncode, so a variant that died after its first binner wrote a directory had
			those bins scored by CheckM and AMBER and ranked - appearing in the table with a
			quality value and cost "unmeasured", which is also what a finished arm whose
			rusage was lost looks like.
		Failed variants are now skipped and named, with --score-failed to score them anyway.
	Fixed - abundance was scored over the intersection of the table and the truth
		A bin the quantifier left out of its table was dropped from both sides rather than
			charged a zero, so omitting a hard bin made the arm's correlation better. It also
			made two arms incomparable: the CoverM path pre-seeds a row per bin while the
			salmon path writes only the rows salmon emitted, so one could be scored over 40
			bins and the other over 200 with nothing in the table saying so.
		Missing bins now score zero - which is what read_abundance_table already does with
			salmon's own `nan` cells - and are reported as abundance_missing_bins.
	Fixed - the depth table's samtools pipeline could deadlock with no output and no timeout
		`samtools view | samtools depth` ran with both stderr pipes unread until the depth
			stream had been fully consumed. These are per-read tools over hundreds of millions
			of reads: one warning per record fills the 64 KiB pipe buffer, samtools blocks on
			the write, stops producing rows, and the read of its stdout never returns. Reading
			the pipes afterwards is too late - the deadlock happens while they are being read.
		Both are now drained by their own thread, started before anything reads stdout, and a
			failure of the second Popen no longer leaves the first writing into a pipe nobody
			will read. The regression test uses real subprocesses, since the defect is in the
			pipes rather than in the logic: it hangs indefinitely against the old code.
	Fixed - the disk monitor could abort the step after the one it was watching
		It sampled, then interrupted the main thread without re-checking whether the run had
			been stopped in the meantime - and sampling is two statvfs calls and an os.stat,
			which on a stalled mount is seconds. Under `run` or `cohort` the KeyboardInterrupt
			landed in the next step, which was recorded as interrupted, while the step actually
			being watched was reported as a disk stop it never hit.
		stop() and the decision to interrupt now take the same lock, so one strictly precedes
			the other.
	Fixed - `doctor` silently stopped reporting the opt-in environments
		It found each opt-in env's owning module with env.split("-")[1], which worked only
			while they were all named metawrap2-<module>-<tool>. Consolidating the three
			learned binners into metawrap2-learned left that with no second dash, so the owner
			came out empty, every opt-in env was filtered out, and the section printed nothing
			- without an error. Replaced by a stated owner table, which a test ties to
			OPTIONAL_ENVS so an env added without an owner fails rather than disappears.
	Fixed - stale install instructions in the binning module's own help
		--semibin2/--vamb/--comebin each told the user to run `install-env binning-semibin2`
			and so on, which resolve to nothing since those three envs became metawrap2-learned.
			They now name `install-env learned`.
	Changed - the test suite pins use_conda_envs itself rather than inheriting it
		Most tests fake the tool call and want no env wrapping; a handful are about how an env
			is resolved and mark themselves @pytest.mark.conda_envs. Left to the ambient config
			the two groups could not both pass, and which did depended on whether CI wrote a
			config file - so the suite passed in CI and failed on a developer's machine. Pinned
			per test in conftest, and the CI and pre-push steps no longer write a config at all.
	Changed - documentation consolidated into docs/, and the benchmark report published with it
		installation/ (three files still describing one conda env per module, naming envs that
			no longer exist), Module_descriptions.md and Usage_tutorial.md were a second copy of
			docs/installation.md, docs/modules.md and docs/usage.md that had drifted. Removed;
			docs/ is what mkdocs builds and the only prose documentation.
		WORK_SUMMARY.md is now docs/benchmarks.md and in the nav, since it is the evidence
			behind every default that moved. Its open decision about the shipped tier is
			settled above, and the review findings in this release are recorded in its own
			defects section - including which committed figures need a re-score.
	Known issue - the identity floor's denominator is the query length, not the aligned length
		`qlen` in a samtools filter expression counts soft-clipped and inserted bases, where
			jgi's percentIdentity divides by the aligned length alone. For a 150 bp read with
			90 bp soft-clipped, --min-identity 90 permits NM <= 15 over 60 aligned bases, about
			75% identity - and minimap2 -ax sr, the default aligner, soft-clips routinely. So
			the effective floor is looser than the number asked for.
		Not changed here: it agrees with jgi to Pearson 0.9992 on the means over all 210,632
			CAMI2 contigs, so the aggregate effect is small, and tightening it moves every
			depth table and so every binning result. Recorded on _identity_expression and in
			the benchmark report's limitations as a change to re-measure deliberately.
	Removed - "binning --subsample" and "coverage --subsample", which were mine and were not useful
		Thinning reads before the coverage alignment looks like free speed, because alignment
			is the whole cost of measuring coverage. Measured on CAMI2 GI with metaBAT2: half
			the reads keeps 33 of 37 high-quality MAGs for 53% of the step's cpu, a quarter
			keeps 25, a tenth keeps 12. The trade is better than proportional - and the
			denominator is wrong. Binning is under 2% of a default run, so halving it saves
			about 1% of the pipeline and gives up 11% of the genomes.
		Read capping where it does pay is reassemble_bins --max-coverage, which is the request
			this was scope creep against: there the input is per bin and an abundant organism
			really does hand SPAdes hundreds of times the coverage it can use.
		The measurement is kept as a comment in modules/_common.py beside the aligner helper,
			so the idea is not re-invented, pointing out that reads thinned before *assembly*
			would be thinning the 86% rather than the 2%. The read_subsample benchmark
			comparison and its tests go with the flag.
	Removed - the depth-variance ablation is no longer presented as a result
		It answered an internal question - whether an alignment-free coverage estimator is
			disqualified by metaBAT2 wanting a variance column - and the answer is a property
			of metaBAT2, not a MetaWrap2 capability. Four depth tables with identical means and
			contigs, differing only in that column: at three samples the spread is 1.5% of F1
			and the nominally best arm is the one where every contig wears another contig's
			variance; at one sample it is worth about 10%.
		Kept in full as a comment on depth.py's variance bullet, because it is the thing to
			know before building a cheaper contig-abundance estimator: on a cohort such an
			estimator does not have to produce a real variance, it has to get the means right.
	Fixed - a study sheet using any --preset but "fast" paid for binning and then failed at refinement
		run resolved which bin sets to hand bin_refinement by scanning for "--<binner>" flags
			and knew nothing about --preset, so every preset fell through to the default pair
			and refinement was pointed at a maxbin2_bins that binning had never created. It
			failed with "is not a directory" after the expensive step.
		selected_binners now resolves --preset from binning.PRESETS, with the same precedence
			that module applies: an explicitly named binner wins, and the preset is consulted
			only when none was. One table, not two.
	New - the --preset tiers are measured end to end, against metaWRAP 1 on one axis
		The tier figures so far came from binning CAMI2's gold assembly, which isolates the
			binner and is the right way to compare binners - and is not what a user gets. These
			arms assemble per sample like the metaWRAP 1 comparison, reusing that comparison's
			assemblies rather than recomputing them, since assembly is 86% of a run and is
			identical across tiers. Each tier's total is the shared read_qc + assembly plus its
			own measured binning and refinement.
		  tier                 HQ   clean   cpu-h
		  very-fast            39     36     92.1
		  fast (shipped)       45     41     97.1
		  metaWRAP 1.3.0       52     48    132.0
		  default              56     53    127.1
		  slow                 61     57    164.1
		The shipped tier loses to metaWRAP 1. "default" returns 53 clean MAGs for 127
			cpu-hours - more genomes than metaWRAP 1 for less compute, the first configuration
			here that beats the baseline on both axes - and "slow" returns 57, a 19% gain for
			24% more cpu.
		Worth noting how far the two designs disagree: on the gold assembly the best tier beats
			the shipped one by 3x, and end to end by 1.4x. What a binner can do and what it does
			for a user are not the same number, and quoting the first for the second would have
			overstated the tiers by more than twice.
	New - "binning --preset {very-fast,fast,default,slow}", binner tiers chosen by compute budget
		"Which binners should be supported" and "which should run" are different
			questions, and a single default answered only the first. Measured on CAMI2 as clean
			high-quality MAGs - bins that are both >=70/<=10 and non-chimeric by GUNC, counted
			per bin rather than estimated as a product:
		  very-fast  metaBAT2              37 HQ   35 clean   94.6%    4.8 cpu-h
		  fast       metaBAT2 + MaxBin2    71 HQ   59 clean   83.1%    7.1
		  default    metaBAT2 + SemiBin2  131 HQ  114 clean   87.0%   86.8 (17 on a GPU)
		  slow       COMEBin              178 HQ  176 clean   98.9%   39.6 (on a GPU)
		Each tier costs more than the one below and recovers more clean MAGs. They are not
			nested; see the correction below for why that property was withdrawn rather than
			designed away. Naming binners explicitly still wins over a preset.
		Corrected: slow was metaBAT2 + SemiBin2 + COMEBin, on a comparison against a figure
			that was wrong. COMEBin's clean-MAG count had been estimated as 178 HQ bins times
			an 88.8% GUNC pass rate - but GUNC had run over all 376 bins COMEBin emitted, 198
			of which never passed 70/10 and were never MAG candidates, so that percentage was
			partly about bins nobody was counting. The ensemble's 96.7% was undiluted, its GUNC
			having run on an already-consolidated 183-bin set, so the two were never comparable.
		Counted per bin (bench_work/clean_hq.py), 176 of COMEBin's 178 high-quality bins pass
			GUNC - 98.9%, the cleanest set measured here. The ensemble's real margin is one bin,
			177 against 176, for 33% more cpu. That is a tie, and a tie goes to the cheaper arm.
		The same pattern appears a tier down: SemiBin2 alone on a GPU recovers 116 clean MAGs
			against metaBAT2+SemiBin2's 114, for a fifth of the cpu. Consolidating a strong
			learned binner with weaker classical ones is not reliably worth its cost on this
			data. default keeps the pair anyway, because metaBAT2 reads the same depth table
			SemiBin2 does and costs ~1.3 cpu-hours beside it, which is a fair price for not
			betting a whole run on one model. --metabat2 --semibin2 --comebin still works.
		The shipped default is unchanged for now: making "default" the fallback would
			make ~1.5 GB of pytorch a requirement of a plain run, which is a decision about
			the install and not about binning.
	Changed - bin_refinement does not score the same bin twice, or score a bin it can rule out
		Refinement scores every combination of its input bin sets, and CheckM is nearly all
			of what that costs. Two measured redundancies in it, both removable without
			changing a single reported number:
		  - 32% of the 1,230 bins across the seven combinations of a three-binner run are
			    the same bin again. A bin is now keyed by a hash of its sorted contig ids, so
			    a bin whose contents have been scored before reuses that score. Some bins
			    appear in all seven sets. The cache is written to work_files, so --resume
			    keeps it.
		  - 0.7% of raw binner bins hold 11.8% of all the sequence, and they are the ones
			    whose size already rules them out. metaBAT2's unbinnable remainder reaches
			    159 Mb; no prokaryotic genome is. Bins outside 200 kb to 20 Mb are recorded
			    as 0% complete and 100% contaminated without being scored. CheckM's cost
			    follows sequence rather than bin count, which is why 0.7% of bins is worth
			    this much.
		The bounds are calibrated against the 18,490 bins in this project's benchmarks that
			have real CheckM2 scores - bench_work/prefilter_calib.py - not guessed. Of the
			10,738 that pass 70/10 the smallest is 0.99 Mb and the largest 6.66 Mb, so both
			bounds are clear of the real distribution, and 20 Mb is a biological ceiling
			rather than a margin fitted to this data: the largest known prokaryotic genomes
			are ~16 Mb. Applying them skips 251 bins and loses zero passing bins.
			"--score-everything" turns the filter off; the cache has no reason to be turned
			off, since a bin with the same contigs has the same completeness.
		The pre-filter saves nothing in bin_refinement, which is where it was added first,
			and that only became visible on splitting the calibration by call site:
			copy_bins already holds every input bin to 50 kb - 20 Mb, so of 10,168 bins
			scored inside refinement none is over the ceiling and 56 - 0.02% of the sequence
			- fall under the floor. The 11.8% is all in raw binner output. So it is wired
			into benchmark.assess.run_bin_quality too, which scores bin sets as the binner
			emitted them and is where those bins actually are. Left in both places because
			it costs nothing and neither call site should depend on the other's filtering.
		An N50 floor was implemented, measured, and dropped. A bin of thousands of tiny
			contigs looks obviously unusable, and the data disagrees: the worst N50 among
			passing bins is 2,025 bp, so a 2 kb floor clears the real distribution by 25 bp
			and buys 1.4 percentage points more sequence than the size bounds alone, while a
			2.5 kb floor discards 36 bins that pass 70/10. A floor low enough to be safe
			skips nothing the size bounds had not. Fragmentation is a property of the
			assembly, not evidence that a bin is several organisms.
		A skipped bin is recorded as failing rather than omitted, so nothing downstream has
			to guess what a missing row meant, and 0/100 can never win a comparison.
	Fixed - "benchmark score" run from the wrong directory wrote a second results file and said nothing
		The default results path was relative - benchmarks/results/<dataset>.tsv - so scoring
			from anywhere but the repository root wrote a whole results file under wherever
			that happened to be, reported success, and left the committed one untouched. The
			numbers in it were real and had to be merged back by hand. A committed results TSV
			exists so that a moved default is a diff rather than a recollection, and this made
			that invisible.
		It is now anchored to the registry, and therefore to the checkout, the same way
			datasets.toml and configurations.toml already were.
	Fixed - a re-score that changed which bin set is an arm's answer was added to the old number
		scoring's dedup key included the source filename, so the stale row and the new one
			keyed differently and were summed rather than one replacing the other. After the
			fix below made refined sets visible, COMEBin's 178 high-quality MAGs read as 356.
		Same dataset, comparison, variant, sample and metric is one measurement whatever file
			it was read out of. A figure that two bin sets contribute to additively is suffixed
			per set long before it reaches the ranker, which is what the suffix is for.
	Fixed - every multi-binner arm went unranked, because its consolidated set was one level down
		A binning comparison's variants are refined as a second step, which writes into a
			subdirectory of the variant. assess.bin_dirs only scanned the variant root, so six
			arms of the binner comparison - including every ensemble worth having - reported
			"left several raw bin sets and no consolidated one" and were recorded per bin set
			but never ranked. The refusal to rank overlapping raw sets is right; the premise
			was wrong, because the consolidated set was sitting in refined/.
		A consolidated set one level down is now found. Only refined names are taken from a
			subdirectory: refinement restores its input sets beside its output under their
			original names, so accepting any *_bins from one level down would pull in a second
			copy of the raw sets and double-count every genome in them.
		This is why the binner table had to be read out of .stats.tsv files by hand to be
			read at all, and why "metabat2_semibin2: 105 clean MAGs" had to be quoted from
			somewhere other than `benchmark score`.
	Fixed - with one input bin set, metawrap_70_10_bins held every bin rather than the ones that pass
		Consolidation applies the -c/-x thresholds as a side effect of merging bin sets, so
			with two or more inputs the final folder contains only bins that passed. With
			exactly one there is nothing to merge, and nothing had ever applied the
			thresholds: the folder was filled with every input bin under a name that states
			one. Refining a single COMEBin run put 376 bins in metawrap_70_10_bins, of which
			178 pass 70/10 - and a 0%-complete bin in a folder named for a quality floor is a
			number someone will report.
		refinement.select_passing is that filter on its own, sharing one predicate
			(refinement.passes) with consolidation so the two paths cannot drift apart on a
			bin that lands exactly on a threshold.
	New - a fourth arm for the quantifier comparison: "depth", and it is measured
		The depth quantifier shipped with a cost argument and no accuracy number. Its arm is
			handed the depth table the binning run wrote - benchmark.runner.depth_table_for
			finds it - rather than deriving a fresh one, which would be measuring the aligner
			again under another name.
		  quantifier   Spearman  Pearson   L1 sim   cpu-hours
		  sylph          0.868    0.811    0.722      0.126
		  salmon         0.827    0.959    0.835      1.078
		  CoverM         0.803    0.952    0.791      2.368
		  depth          0.780    0.953    0.799      0.013
		It splits the two metrics: second of four on magnitude, ahead of CoverM on both Pearson
			and L1, and last on ordering - which is the metric to lean on. A coherent split
			rather than noise, since Pearson is dominated by the abundant bins while Spearman
			weights every bin alike, so a method slightly noisy on low-coverage bins reads well
			on one and badly on the other.
		Worth having for magnitudes when the table is already on disk - it costs 0.013 cpu-hours
			against sylph's 0.126 and CoverM's 2.368 - and not a candidate for the default.
	New - "quant_bins --quantifier depth", bin abundance from the table binning already built
		The other three quantifiers align the reads a second time to answer a question
			the binning run already answered, and answer it on a different scale. This
			aggregates the per-contig depth table into a length-weighted mean per bin -
			total bases over total length - with the per-contig median beside it.
		No reads, no index, no alignment: one pass over a TSV against the 0.2 to 2.4
			cpu-hours the others spend, and on the binner's own scale rather than twice it.
	Changed - the alignment-identity floor for a depth table is 90, not jgi's 97
		Inherited from jgi_summarize_bam_contig_depths, never measured here, and known from
			the fidelity work to be a 2x lever - which made it the largest unexamined
			parameter in the pipeline. bench_work/identity_sweep/ is the experiment: one set
			of minimap2 alignments, six depth tables from it at different floors, metaBAT2 on
			each, AMBER against CAMI2's truth on the gold assembly. The arms share the
			alignments, so the floor is the only difference.
		  floor   mean depth   bins   AMBER F1   purity   HQ   clean HQ
		  none        15.71      90     0.6607   0.8374     48      45
		  90 (now)    15.70      88     0.6624   0.8375     48      47
		  95          13.46      86     0.6574   0.8264     43      40
		  97 (was)     8.35      74     0.6371   0.8175     37      35
		  99           1.48      21     0.2452   0.7904      -       -
		  100            -        7     0.0525   0.9998      -       -
		Against the true genomes - the gold assembly is the reference genomes - the 97% floor
			keeps 53% of the depth no filter keeps. It loses 14 bins of 88 and gains nothing,
			and it loses purity as well as completeness. That last part answers the obvious
			objection: a looser floor was supposed to let a relative's reads inflate a
			contig's depth and merge two strains into one bin, buying completeness at
			purity's expense. At a 90% floor purity is higher. The filter is not separating
			strains here, it is removing signal the binner was using.
		The 99 and 100 rows are the mechanism, not candidates. At 150 bp a 1% error rate puts
			a typical read near 99% identity, so a 99% floor cuts most reads on error alone;
			purity 0.9998 at 100 means 7 bins covering 3.2% of the assembly are each nearly
			pure, which is the metric rewarding a binner for binning almost nothing.
		GUNC had to agree before this could move a default, because AMBER purity is
			bp-weighted: a bin that is 95% one genome and 5% another costs almost nothing, and
			that property already picked the wrong default once in this project. It agrees, and
			more strongly than AMBER did: on the project's own metric - bins that are both
			>=70/<=10 and non-chimeric, counted per bin - a 90% floor recovers 47 clean
			high-quality MAGs against 97's 35, a 34% gain, and has the highest clean fraction
			of any arm at 97.9%. Over the raw bin sets without a completeness filter the same
			pattern holds: 82 non-chimeric bins against 65, chimera rate 6.8% against 12.2%.
			Two independent chimera detectors say the same thing.
		90 rather than no filter at all, because a floor still has a job - a read 20%
			divergent from a contig is evidence about some other organism - and because 0 and
			90 are within 0.002 AMBER F1 of each other, so the insurance is free.
		"binning --min-identity" and "coverage --min-identity" expose it, and
			depth.JGI_PERCENT_IDENTITY stays named: --min-identity 97 reproduces a jgi table,
			which is what comparing against metaWRAP 1 or against metaBAT2's own published
			validation needs.
		Measured on one dataset, one binner, and the gold assembly. A looser floor should
			matter less on a self-assembled contig set, because a fragmented consensus already
			absorbs some of the strain variation the true genomes do not - so this is the
			change here most worth re-testing elsewhere, and the number is a constant and a
			flag rather than a literal.
	Changed - the very-fast tier's cost was a bwa-era figure, and is now measured: 4.80 cpu-hours
		metaBAT2 alone was last measured when bwa was the default aligner, and the 22.9
			cpu-hours in the preset table was almost entirely that alignment. Re-run under
			minimap2 it is 4.80 - a factor of 4.8, which is bwa's measured overhead.
	New - "benchmarks/coverage_fidelity.py" checks the estimators per contig, not by MAG count
		Every coverage judgement here had been indirect: run a binner, count the MAGs.
			This compares each estimator to bwa contig by contig on one assembly, and
			reports three things that fail independently - agreement, units, and how often
			a method calls a contig absent that bwa finds coverage for.
		Findings, in full in bench_work/cov_fidelity/FINDINGS.md:
		  - No method reports an unnormalised k-mer total. For all of them the
			    correlation against depth x length is far below that against depth, and a
			    method that had skipped dividing by length would show the reverse. fairy's
			    columns are a genuine per-base depth.
		  - CoverM reads 2.001x bwa because jgi filters reads below 97% alignment
			    identity and CoverM does not. Same BAM, filter off: 60.49 mean depth
			    against 31.24 filtered, and CoverM reports 60.44. On this gut metagenome
			    that filter discards half the mapped bases. CoverM is therefore not a
			    drop-in for a jgi depth table, and the 97% threshold is an unevaluated
			    lever with a 2x effect.
		  - fairy's in-sample depths are respectable (Pearson 0.946) and its cohort
			    table is not: 20.6% of contigs have two or more non-zero samples against
			    the aligned route's 58.5%. That alone explains B5 arm d losing 10 of 37
			    MAGs, and it follows from fairy's own -m 95 ANI floor.
	Fixed - "clean HQ MAGs" was estimated as a product, and the estimate was wrong where it mattered
		The headline metric is the number of bins that are *both* >=70/<=10 *and* non-chimeric
			by GUNC. It had been computed as "HQ count x GUNC pass fraction", which is the same
			number only when GUNC ran over exactly the bins that pass 70/10.
		Often it had not. An arm whose GUNC ran over a raw binner bin set has its pass fraction
			diluted by bins that were never MAG candidates: COMEBin emits 376 bins of which 178
			pass 70/10, so its 88.8% was partly a statement about the other 198. An arm whose
			GUNC ran over a consolidated 70/10-filtered set has the undiluted figure. One column
			held both kinds, and both are plausible percentages, so nothing looked wrong.
		bench_work/clean_hq.py joins the CheckM and GUNC reports by bin name and counts the
			intersection, which needs no assumption about what either was run over. The
			corrections are large where the dilution was: COMEBin 158 -> 176, MaxBin2 17 -> 33,
			CONCOCT 19 -> 28, SemiBin2 on a GPU 109 -> 116. Arms whose GUNC already ran on a
			filtered set are unchanged.
		It changed a default. See the --preset entry: the three-binner ensemble's margin over
			COMEBin alone was 19 clean MAGs on the estimate and is one bin on the count.
	Fixed - make_figures summed an arm's re-measurements instead of taking the latest
		The same defect as in the scorer's dedup, in the other consumer of the results TSV: an
			arm re-run under a different aligner had both costs summed, so metaBAT2's cpu-hours
			read 27.7 - its bwa-era 22.9 plus its 4.8 re-measurement - for a run that cost 4.8.
		load() now keys each metric by sample and keeps the last row per sample, so totals sum
			across samples (ten samples really do cost ten samples' cpu-hours) and never across
			re-measurements of one sample.
	Changed - nothing is ranked on MAGs per cpu-hour any more, including the scorer itself
		The ratio is dominated by whichever arm is cheapest rather than by which
			recovers the most genomes, and following it produced a default that recovers
			fewer MAGs than metaWRAP 1 does - 45 against 52 end to end.
		Rankings are now on clean high-quality MAG count, with cost reported in a
			column beside it. Where cost has to drive a choice it is a tier the user picks
			(--preset) rather than a ratio that picks for them.
		"benchmark score" ranks on the quality metric and prints cost beside it.
			scoring.time_to_performance is now value_against_cost, its Ratio is a Scored, and
			the type has no "ratio" property at all - when there was one, it was what
			everything got ranked by. Cost breaks a tie between arms that scored identically
			and does nothing else. An arm whose cost was never recorded now ranks on its
			quality instead of being pushed to the bottom of the table by a bookkeeping gap.
		The same framing came out of the module docstrings, the benchmark configurations
			and docs/, which between them had stated the old rule in fourteen places - a
			default is only as reviewable as the rule stated next to it.
	New - "mw2", the same CLI under a shorter name
		Every command here is typed as "metawrap2 <module> <flags>", and the prefix is the
			part nobody needs to read. "mw2" is the same entry point, not a wrapper.
	Changed - one conda env per workflow, not one per module: 22 environments became 9
		Modules that run in sequence and share tools now share an environment. read_qc,
			assembly, reassemble_bins and blobology are one env; binning, coverage,
			dereplicate and quant_bins are another. Five workflow envs, plus four that stay
			separate for a stated reason.
		Every grouping was solved against conda-forge + bioconda before being written
			down. Two splits are forced by the solver rather than chosen: CheckM2 has no
			common solution with CheckM1 + GUNC + DAS Tool, and GTDB-Tk none with
			taxator-tk. The third, the learned binners, is a size decision - pytorch is
			~1.5 GB and nobody who only wants metaBAT2 should wait for it.
		GUNC, DAS Tool, CoverM, fairy and Bakta are no longer opt-in installs. Keeping
			them out bought nothing: GUNC runs by default unless --skip-gunc, so a default
			step needed a second install command, and the other four are single binaries or
			solve with what they sit beside.
		SemiBin2, VAMB and COMEBin share one env where they had three. They are all
			pytorch applications and they solve together, so that was three copies of one
			~1.5 GB dependency graph.
		"install-env --all" builds each env once rather than once per module that uses
			it, and "install-env bins" / "doctor bins" now resolve an env name as well as a
			module name - without that, doctor reported a freshly built shared env as
			"0/0 tools OK", a pass that had probed nothing, and install-env --lock said an
			env that existed did not.
	Changed - the contig depth table is computed here, not by jgi_summarize_bam_contig_depths
		metawrap2.depth builds the contig x sample table every binner depends on, from
			per-base depths out of "samtools depth". Same algorithm: the same 97% identity
			filter, the same 75 bp trimmed from each contig end, the same intra-contig
			depth variance.
		Verified against the tool it replaces on the same three BAMs over all 210,632
			contigs of the CAMI2 GI assembly: Pearson 0.9992 on every per-sample mean,
			0.9954-0.9963 on the variances, median relative difference about 5%.
		The reason is not that the tool is wrong but that this number is the most
			load-bearing one in the pipeline and was the one thing the pipeline could not
			look inside. When MaxBin2 was found to be binning on invented depths, the only
			way to see it was to run a C++ binary twice and notice the answer changed.
		Two bugs surfaced while checking it against the original, both now fixed here:
			"samtools depth -a" says nothing at all about a reference with no reads, so
			counting emitted positions made a contig's divisor depend on which samples
			happened to cover it - a 4 Mbp contig reported a depth of 1.6 million. The
			divisor is the contig's trimmed length, which is what jgi uses too.
	Fixed - a contig of nothing but N broke VAMB and was silently binned by everything else
		CAMI2's gold-standard gastrointestinal assembly carries 20 records with no A, C,
			G, T or U in them at all, 28.9 Mbp in total and the largest 6.6 Mbp - reference
			genomes masked out, not assembly artefacts.
		Such a record has a zero tetranucleotide vector and no read can align to it, so
			there is no assignment of it that could be right. VAMB refuses to start, which
			is how this was found; metaBAT2, MaxBin2 and CONCOCT take it and silently carry
			a contig they cannot place.
		binning and coverage now leave them out, which makes the binners agree on their
			input and costs nothing.
	New - "binning --device", one switch for whether the learned binners use a GPU
		Replaces --semibin2-engine and --comebin-device, and reaches VAMB, which had no
			way to ask at all. "cpu", "cuda", "cuda:N" or "auto"; auto asks that binner's
			own env whether torch can see a device and falls back to the CPU.
		The default is still cpu, and that is a reproducibility decision rather than a
			statement about the hardware: a GPU run and a CPU run of the same seeded network
			need not agree, so the same study re-analysed elsewhere would produce different
			MAGs for no reason its author could see.
		What that default costs is now measured rather than assumed, by the semibin2_gpu,
			vamb_gpu and comebin_gpu benchmark arms. VAMB is 13.8x cheaper on the GPU (4.9
			cpu-hours against 66.9) and SemiBin2 4.9x (17.3 against 85.5), on three CAMI2
			samples with the same seeds and the same env - only the device differs.
		COMEBin is the extreme case: on CPU it reached epoch 20 of 200 after
			305 cpu-hours, a projected ~1,485 for one arm against 7.1 for metaBAT2 and
			MaxBin2 together. On the CPU it is not slow, it does not finish.
	New - "reassemble_bins --max-coverage", so one abundant genome stops setting the pace
		Read recruitment is per bin, so an abundant organism hands SPAdes hundreds of
			times the coverage it can use - and SPAdes time grows with reads, not with
			genome size. Each bin's recruited reads are thinned to 50x of that bin's own
			assembled length before reassembly; --max-coverage 0 keeps every read.
		Thinned in pairs with a fixed seed, so a re-run recruits and keeps the same
			reads and SPAdes is never handed a library with one mate missing.
	Changed - every command's help states its own defaults and the values it will accept
		One help formatter for all two dozen entry points. A default is read off the
			parser rather than written into the help string a second time, where it drifted;
			a numeric option declares its range once and both checks and prints it.
		So "-c/--completeness" now reads "[0-100] [default: 70]" and rejects -5 at the
			parser instead of somewhere downstream. A help string that explains its own
			default is left alone: "default minimap2; see ALIGNERS for why" says more than
			"[default: minimap2]" and must not be followed by a second copy of it.
	Changed - CONCOCT is no longer run with its parallelism multiplied by its thread count
		"concoct -t 96" forks 96 workers and does array maths in each, and each worker's
			OpenBLAS then started 96 threads of its own. The numerical libraries are now
			capped to one thread per worker so CONCOCT's own -t is the only parallelism.
		Measured: 23.8 cpu-hours against 29.9 on one CAMI2 sample, a 1.3x saving, for a
			bin set identical down to the count. Applied to CONCOCT alone - the learned
			binners are internally threaded, so capping them would make them slower.
		What this does not fix, stated so nobody reads it as closed: the same CONCOCT
			version with byte-identical arguments and input costs 5.6 cpu-hours under a
			Python 2.7 + MKL environment. The cap closes a fifth of that gap. The rest is
			somewhere in the compiled numerical stack and is still open. It only affects
			the --concoct path; the default binner set costs 1.8 cpu-hours.
	Fixed - the temp-directory budget for CheckM1 was 14 characters too generous
		multiprocessing creates a "pymp-XXXXXXXX" subdirectory before putting
			"listener-XXXXXXXX" inside it, and only the second was counted - so a
			77-character temp directory was judged short enough and still died with the bare
			EOFError this guard exists to prevent. It took both arms of the consolidator
			comparison with it.
		The budget is now 75 characters, found by binding real sockets at every length
			from 60 to 90 rather than derived, and pinned by a test that fails at 76.
	Changed - "metawrap2 coverage --coverage fairy" is now measured, and it lost
		Benchmark B5 over three CAMI2 samples, every other token held identical: fairy
			builds the cohort depth table for 0.04 cpu-hours against minimap2's 2.26, 57x
			cheaper, and the dereplicated catalogue that comes out holds 27 high-quality
			MAGs against 37, with 76% of its bins passing GUNC against 87%.
		Its multi-sample claim was the one thing it had left - the single-sample loss was
			already on record at AMBER F1 0.212 against 0.452 - so this closes the route as
			a default candidate rather than leaving it merely unmeasured. It stays opt-in.
		What it gets wrong is its mean depths (Pearson 0.237 against bwa), not the
			variance column: the ablation below shows metaBAT2 gets nothing from that at
			this sample count. An alignment-free estimator with accurate means would still
			be worth having; fairy is not it.
	New - benchmark B5 settles what cross-sample coverage is worth
		Binning each sample against a cohort-wide depth table recovers 37 high-quality
			MAGs against 32 for a sample's own reads alone - 16% more - for 1.9 cpu-hours
			more out of 90, which is 2%. That is the case for the coverage module.
		The mechanism is visible per step: binning drops from 1.81 cpu-hours to 0.63
			because it reads a table rather than aligning, and the coverage step's 2.26 is
			what buys the other samples' depths. The saving should widen with more samples -
			N alignments against N-squared - and three samples is the smallest cohort worth
			the name, so this is a floor rather than a best case.
		Run on three samples rather than the ten originally planned: ten needed ~300 GB
			against a 70% disk ceiling, and three matches every other measurement here.
	New - benchmark B5 measured: cross-sample coverage against single-sample and co-assembly
		Four arms over three CAMI2 samples, each differing in one thing, each assembling
			its own reads, every count after dereplication.
		Cross-sample coverage beats single-sample, which is what the coverage module
			exists for: 37 high-quality MAGs against 32, 23 near-complete against 19, 46
			genomes against 40, for 1.9 cpu-hours more out of 90. The depth table costs 2.26
			and hands 1.2 back, because binning reads a table instead of aligning.
		Against co-assembly it is a wash at this scale, which the plan did not assume:
			co-assembly was cheaper (85.1 cpu-hours against 90.9) and had more bins at 70/10
			(40 against 37), while multi-sample had more near-complete ones (23 against 18)
			and cleaner bins (87.0% passing GUNC against 81.4%). Weighted for chimerism the
			two are level. The cost argument for cohorts is one about scaling, and three
			samples does not demonstrate it.
		fairy, finally settled: supplying the same cohort table from k-mer sketches is
			53x cheaper for that step (0.04 cpu-hours against 2.26) and costs 10 of the 37
			MAGs and half the near-complete ones, with dirtier bins. Its multi-sample claim
			was the one thing it had left and it was measured in exactly the setting it was
			supposed to win in. It stays opt-in, now for a measured reason.
	Fixed - a co-assembly that skipped read QC had no reads to assemble
		The ALL_READS files a co-assembly reads were built only after a read_qc step, so
			a study sheet with coassemble = true and no read_qc in its steps planned an
			assembly whose inputs nothing created - and failed on "does not exist", naming
			the files but not the cause. Every arm of a benchmark that holds read QC
			constant is exactly that shape, which is how it was found.
		The concatenation now happens before the assembly step instead. With read QC the
			files are already current by then and it is a no-op; without it, the raw reads
			are concatenated. clean_reads_for already knew which reads to use - it was
			simply never asked in this case.
	Fixed - an assembly with a zero-length record broke DAS_Tool and blamed the contigs
		DAS_Tool reads the assembly with R's fread, and a header with an empty sequence
			yields one field where it expects two. That stops the read part way and DAS_Tool
			then reports every contig past that point as "not found in assembly" - 36,854 of
			them in the run that found this, in a message naming none of the real problem.
		bin_refinement now drops such records from the copy it hands a consolidator, the
			way binning already did for its own working copy. CAMI2's gold-standard
			assembly ships two of them.
		One input problem is left and is not ours: bins from a *pooled* gold assembly can
			include a 159 Mb "bin" - metaBAT2's unbinnable remainder, not a genome - and
			DAS_Tool's gene prediction fails on it. Per-sample bins, which is what a user
			produces, are fine: 0.62 cpu-hours and 10 bins at 70/10 against the metawrap
			consolidator's 4.52 and 13.
	Fixed - a bin_refinement comparison could not run a variant that needs the assembly
		DAS Tool reads the assembly contigs itself and refuses to start without them, so
			the consolidator comparison could never run its DAS Tool arm. The harness now
			passes -a to every variant of a bin_refinement comparison when the dataset has
			an assembly - to every variant rather than only the ones that ask, because an
			unused -a changes nothing and arms handed different inputs are not a comparison.
	Fixed - CONCOCT burned several times the CPU it needed to
		It forks ``-t`` workers and does array maths in each, so without being told
			otherwise every worker's OpenBLAS started its own thread per core. Capping the
			numerical libraries to one thread for that call leaves CONCOCT's own -t as the
			only parallelism.
		Measured on one CAMI2 sample: 29.9 cpu-hours and 1476 s wall before, 23.8 and
			1248 s after, with identical bin counts. A 1.3x saving - real, and much smaller
			than the 5x gap that prompted the search, so most of that gap is still open. The
			remaining suspect is the build: CONCOCT 1.1.0 for Python 3.12 against
			scikit-learn 1.9, where the same version under Python 2.7 costs 5.6 cpu-hours.
	Fixed - a bin_refinement benchmark comparison never passed an assembly, so DAS Tool could not run
		The command builder was already right - ``if inputs.assembly`` - but nothing ever
			filled the field in for a bin_refinement comparison, so the consolidator arm that
			needs the contigs exited in one second while the arm that does not need them
			passed.
	Fixed - DAS Tool refused CAMI2's gold standard, blaming 36,854 contigs that were all present
		"Contigs of contig2bin files not found in assembly: 36854", where a direct check
			finds every one of them in the assembly. DAS_Tool reads it with R's fread, and a
			record with a header and no sequence yields a line with one field where two are
			expected - which stops the read and reports everything after it as missing.
		bin_refinement now drops those records from the copy it hands a consolidator, as
			binning already did for its own working copy. CAMI2's gold standard ships two.
	Fixed - CheckM1 stopped finding its reference data whenever its environment was rebuilt
		CheckM1 remembers the path in a file inside its own conda env, written by
			``checkm data setRoot``. Consolidating the environments replaced the env it had
			been pointed at, and bin_refinement began dying in CheckM's tree stage with an
			EOFError from a worker - on a machine where nothing about the database had
			changed and metawrap2.toml still named it correctly.
		Every CheckM1 call now carries CHECKM_DATA_PATH from the configured CHECKM_DB,
			which CheckM reads in preference to its stored path. install-db still runs
			setRoot, so `checkm` by hand works too, and it resolves the env from MODULE_ENVS
			rather than spelling out a name that a rename can orphan.
	Fixed - doctor and install-env did not recognise an environment name several modules share
		`doctor bins` resolved to no known tools and reported a freshly built env as
			"0/0 tools OK" - a pass that had probed nothing, from the one command whose job
			is to verify a build, and the check install-env runs after building. It now
			probes the union of the tools of every module sharing that env, because a shared
			env is only healthy if all of them can run.
		`install-env bins --lock` likewise insisted an env that existed did not: the name
			is neither a module nor an opt-in env, so it resolved to the bare word rather
			than to metawrap2-bins.
	Fixed - the benchmark scorer crashed on GUNC after the environments were consolidated
		`assess.gunc_settings` looked GUNC up by the opt-in key it used to have, which
			folding GUNC into bin_refinement's env turned into a KeyError. It fired only on a
			variant whose bins had not already been assessed, so a benchmark chain crashed
			part way through, after the expensive part had succeeded.
		A test now sweeps the source for literal lookups into any of the four env tables
			and fails on one that could not resolve, rather than waiting for the code path
			to be exercised.
	Fixed - doctor reported a correctly installed GUNC database as missing
		GUNC takes --db_file, so install-db points GUNC_DB at the .dmnd itself, and
			every check of a configured database asked whether it was a directory. The one
			database whose correct value cannot be a directory was the one reported broken.
	New - "metawrap2 benchmark", so a default is a measurement rather than an opinion
		"metawrap2 benchmark list|fetch|run|score" measures tools and settings against
			known-truth data and ranks them on accuracy per CPU-hour - not accuracy alone,
			because a variant that scores higher for four times the compute is a better flag
			than a default. Wall time is recorded but is never the denominator: it rewards a
			tool for being given more cores
		datasets and comparisons are data, in benchmarks/datasets.toml and
			benchmarks/configurations.toml, so adding either is an edit rather than a patch.
			The loader refuses a remote file with no checksum or no measured size, and a
			comparison with fewer than two variants or no declared incumbent
		nothing downloads on import, on list, or on run. A fetch requires the dataset to be
			named and its size confirmed with --yes, and refuses outright if it would cross the
			[limits] disk ceiling
		results append to benchmarks/results/<dataset>.tsv in long format, which is committed,
			so a changed default arrives in review next to the numbers for it
		benchmarks/fixtures/tiny/ is a ~370 kB three-genome fixture, committed, so the harness
			itself is testable in under a second without a 95 GB download
		"score" assesses a binning comparison's bins itself, with AMBER and CheckM, so those
			comparisons have an accuracy column at all - a binning run leaves directories of
			FASTA files and had been recording cost against nothing
		assessment happens at score time, outside the measured run, because the cost being
			ranked has to be the cost of the thing being chosen: "metawrap2 binning" does not
			run CheckM, and CheckM's cost grows with the number of bins, so charging it to the
			run would penalise a variant for being productive
		AMBER (Meyer et al., Gigascience 2018) scores bins against the dataset's known
			contig-to-genome truth and is marker-gene independent, which is why it is not
			redundant with CheckM: several binners use single-copy markers internally, and
			scoring those with a marker-based tool asks them to grade their own homework.
			"metawrap2 install-env benchmark-amber" creates its env, and nothing a user runs
			depends on it
		a variant that ran three binners is not scored by adding its three bin sets together.
			They overlap, so the same genome would be counted up to three times and an F1 above
			1 sorts first and looks plausible. The ranked numbers come from the consolidated
			set (metawrap_<c>_<x>_bins) or from a lone set; otherwise they are recorded per set as
			metric__<bin set> and "score" says which variants it declined to rank, and why
		"--reassess" re-runs the assessors over bins that already have results, and
			"--assembly" names the contigs AMBER should take its sequence lengths from (the
			run record already knows, for a run made by "benchmark run")
		"run" and "score" now accept the same spellings for the work directory: -o, --output
			and --work all mean it, in either command
	New - "metawrap2 binning --semibin2"
		SemiBin2 2.5.0 joins metaBAT2, MaxBin2 and CONCOCT as a selectable binner. It learns
			contig embeddings by self-supervised contrastive training rather than modelling
			composition and coverage directly, and it is the only binner here that reads the
			BAMs rather than a depth table - it computes its own abundance features alongside
			the embedding, so "single_easy_bin" is handed --input-bam. Not a default and not
			yet recommended: the binner_set comparison has arms for it (semibin2 and
			metabat2_semibin2) and nothing changes until they have run
		its own env, "metawrap2 install-env binning-semibin2", because it brings pytorch -
			roughly 2-3 GB that the three default binners have no use for, and that a user
			who never asks for SemiBin2 should never download
		--random-seed is fixed and --semibin2-engine defaults to cpu, where SemiBin2 would
			default to "auto" and train on a GPU whenever torch can see one. Found by running
			it on a machine with several GPUs and reading "Running with GPU" in its log. Two
			reasons it is pinned: MetaWrap2 chooses defaults on accuracy per cpu-hour, and a
			binner whose training silently moves to an accelerator is neither measured on the
			same axis as metaBAT2 nor chosen on hardware most users have; and "auto" makes the
			bins depend on the host, so the same study re-analysed elsewhere yields different
			MAGs for no visible reason. "--semibin2-engine gpu" trades that back for speed
		SemiBin2 refuses to run on contigs shorter than 4000 bp - it cannot form must-link
			pairs - so it cannot be exercised on the tiny fixture, and it drops bins under
			200 kb by a floor of its own that -l does not reach
	New - "metawrap2 binning --vamb"
		VAMB 5.0.4 joins as the second learned binner, training a variational autoencoder over
			composition and abundance jointly where SemiBin2 pretrains contrastively on
			composition. It reads the BAM directory (--bamdir) rather than individual BAMs or a
			depth table. Not a default, for the same reason SemiBin2 is not: the binner_set arms
			vamb and metabat2_vamb decide it
		its own env, "metawrap2 install-env binning-vamb", separate from binning-semibin2
			rather than shared with it - both bring pytorch, but they pin different versions, so
			solving them together lets one tool's upgrade break the other
		three of VAMB's defaults are overridden, each of which fails silently if left alone:
			--minfasta has no default at all, and without it VAMB writes a cluster TSV and zero
			FASTA files, which looks from outside like a binner that found nothing (set to
			200 kb, matching SemiBin2's floor); -o is emptied to turn off binsplitting, whose
			default separator "C" splits a bin apart on a letter that appears mid-identifier in
			ordinary contig names; and -m is floored at VAMB's hard minimum of 250 rather than
			its recommended 2000, which would quietly discard contigs -l asked to bin
		VAMB refuses to run into an --outdir that already exists, so the module clears its
			working output first - safe only because the checkpoint has already decided the
			stage is being redone, so nothing in there is a result. --seed is passed despite
			VAMB documenting that it does not guarantee determinism: it removes the variation
			it can remove
		verified against VAMB 5.0.4 on the tiny fixture: it trains and clusters there (257
			contigs into 160 bins) but writes none of them, every one being under the 200 kb
			floor - so like SemiBin2 it cannot be smoke-tested end to end on tiny, for a
			different reason. Bins land in "<outdir>/bins/<n>.fna", confirmed by running it
			with the floor lowered
	New - "metawrap2 binning --comebin"
		COMEBin 1.1.0 joins as the third learned binner, and the only one that selects among its
			own candidate bin sets: it trains contrastively over six augmented views, produces
			several Leiden clusterings, and picks between them by scoring each against
			single-copy marker genes - internally doing something close to what bin_refinement
			does across binners. Entry point is the shell script run_comebin.sh, and like VAMB it
			takes a BAM directory and globs *.bam out of it. Arms comebin and metabat2_comebin
		its own env, "metawrap2 install-env binning-comebin", distinct from the other two
			learned binners' for the same torch-pinning reason, and also carrying FragGeneScan,
			HMMER and pplacer for the marker-gene selection step
		-d defaults to cpu where COMEBin's own default is plain "cuda" - a stronger version of
			SemiBin2's problem, since "auto" at least falls back to the CPU and "cuda" just asks
			torch for a GPU and fails without one. "--comebin-device cuda" opts in
		-s is passed because run_comebin.sh forwards --seed only when the flag is given, so
			COMEBin out of the box is an unseeded network and an unseeded Leiden clustering
		-l is honoured by filtering the input rather than by a flag: run_comebin.sh never
			forwards --contig_len, so COMEBin bins at its built-in 1000 bp whatever was asked
			for. When --min-len is higher the assembly handed to COMEBin is pre-filtered to it,
			so its bins rest on the same contigs every other binner saw; at or below 1000 the
			shared copy is used and nothing is written
		COMEBin needs no clearing between attempts, unlike VAMB: run_comebin.sh checks its own
			intermediate manifests and skips finished stages, so the expensive augmentation pass
			survives a --resume and the output directory is left alone
		verified against COMEBin 1.1.0 on the tiny fixture: it completes augmentation and
			contrastive training on the CPU with the seed honoured (~4 min on 8 threads for 257
			contigs) and then fails in clustering with "HMMsearch completed but found zero marker
			hits", the fixture's synthetic genomes carrying no single-copy markers to seed on. So
			all three learned binners are unexercisable on tiny, each for a different reason
	New - "metawrap2 bin_refinement --consolidator dastool", a second way to reach one bin set
		MetaWrap2's own consolidation hybridises every combination of the input bin sets with
			Binning_refiner and asks CheckM which version of each bin to keep, so CheckM runs on
			2^n-1 sets. DAS_Tool scores the input bins once on single-copy genes and aggregates in
			one pass. That is a different accuracy/cost point, not a different implementation of
			the same idea, so which one is the default is a measurement: the new "consolidator"
			comparison in benchmarks/configurations.toml ranks them on NC MAGs per CPU-hour.
			"metawrap" stays the default because it is the incumbent, not because it has won
		both routes leave one set in binsO, and the whole downstream half - re-scoring with CheckM,
			the -c/-x cut, the ranking plots, the final layout, GUNC - is the same code either
			way. That is what makes the two comparable: the only thing that differs is how the one
			set was arrived at, not what counts as a final bin
		needs -a/--assembly, because DAS_Tool reads the contigs itself. Checked up front, along with
			the env, before a single bin is copied: finding out after prodigal has read every bin
			is the whole cost of the arm for nothing. --skip-consolidation alongside it is
			reported as the contradiction it is rather than resolved by precedence
		the assembly is re-normalised first, and only if it needs it. Bins have been through
			_fix_contig_naming, which rewrites "=" in a contig id, and a consolidator matching
			those names against an unnormalised assembly finds none of them - which is not an
			error anywhere, just an empty consolidation. metaSPAdes and MEGAHIT names normalise to
			themselves, so the common case copies nothing, which matters when assemblies are
			gigabytes
		CheckM is deliberately not run on the input sets for this route. It exists to tell
			MetaWrap2's own consolidation which version of each bin to keep; DAS_Tool decides from
			its own scoring. Running it anyway would add the module's largest cost to the arm that
			was supposed to be the cheap one, and would make the cost comparison meaningless
		its bins are renumbered to bin_001.fasta like every other MetaWrap2 bin set - .stats.tsv
			rows are keyed on those stems - and DAS_Tool's own names, which say which input set
			each bin came from, are kept in work_files/dastool_bin_names.tsv rather than discarded
		--search_engine diamond, not DAS_Tool's default: the default is usearch, which is not free
			software and is not in the env, so the default command fails partway into a run
		verified end-to-end against DAS_Tool 1.1.7 on real CAMI2 bins: 8 input bins across two sets
			consolidated to 7, then cut to 6 by CheckM2 at -c 50 -x 10
		MAGScoT was the other candidate and is not packaged: "mamba repoquery search -c bioconda
			-c conda-forge *magscot*" finds nothing, so it is GitHub/Docker only - the same
			packaging exception as MetaDecoder (PyPI) and binny (a Snakemake workflow), and not
			the small env the plan assumed
	Fixed - the benchmark could not find the consolidated bin set, so no ensemble could be ranked
		"benchmark score" looked for a directory called "metawrap2_bins", which bin_refinement has
			never written: it names its answer for the thresholds it cut at, metawrap_70_10_bins.
			So a refined ensemble arm looked like several raw overlapping bin sets with nothing
			consolidated, and the harness correctly refused to rank it - over an answer that was
			sitting in the same directory. Exactly the comparison the CONCOCT question needs
		one rule now, in metawrap2.constants: refined_bins_dirname() writes the name and
			is_refined_bins_dirname() recognises it, matched on shape rather than against a list,
			because -c 50 -x 5 is as valid as the defaults
		several consolidated sets in one directory - a run resumed at different thresholds - is
			refused rather than resolved by sort order, for the same reason three raw sets are
		the tests pinned the fictional name, which is how this survived. They pin the real one now,
			and pin "metawrap2_bins" as explicitly *not* matching
	New - "metawrap2 assembly --aligner", so the hybrid path's remapping is not hardcoded to bwa
		assembly was the third module that maps reads back to an assembly and the only one still
			spelling its own aligner command out. It now takes --aligner from the same registry
			binning and blobology use (metawrap2.aligners), so the three cannot drift apart on
			which aligners they offer
		the default is bwa, deliberately *not* the registry's minimap2, and a test asserts the
			difference so it cannot be quietly aligned. Measured on the 273k-scaffold metaSPAdes
			assembly from the assembler arm and the 3-sample co-assembly reads behind it: bwa
			5.45 CPU-hours and 2,867,680 reads recruited as unassembled, minimap2 0.62 CPU-hours
			- 8.8x less - and 4,113,486, which is 44% more
		8.8x cheaper and not a drop-in, because the disagreement is one-sided: minimap2 calls
			1,249,798 reads unmapped that bwa placed on a scaffold, against 3,992 the other way
			(Jaccard 0.695). Its sr preset is less sensitive on a fragmented real assembly, so
			those reads reach MEGAHIT labelled unassembled and are assembled a second time, and
			combining the two assemblies cannot tell a duplicate from a novel contig. A cost
			saving cannot buy a change in what the assembly contains. This is the opposite
			conclusion to binning's from the same two tools, because the property read out of the
			alignment is different - which is the argument for measuring per question, not per tool
		the scaffold index is deleted once the FASTQ exists. Nothing else in the module reads an
			alignment and the stage is checkpointed, so a --resume rebuilds it anyway; it is not
			small next to what it was built from - minimap2's .mmi for a 176 MB scaffold set is
			larger than the fasta
		bwa-mem2 and minimap2 are now declared in the assembly env rather than assumed. minimap2
			was already present as a dependency of quast, so --aligner minimap2 would have worked
			without being declared and broken the day quast dropped it
		bwa mem now runs with -v 1, the registry's template, where this module had passed no
			verbosity flag. It changes what bwa writes to stderr and nothing about the alignment
	Fixed - four modules were treating the unbinned leftovers as a candidate genome
		a bin set may carry "unbinned.fasta" and is meant to: binning writes the contigs no
			CONCOCT cluster claimed there on purpose, so a user can see what was left over. Only
			the AMBER scorer knew that. bin_refinement copied it in as a bin, which puts it
			through CheckM, into every pairwise Binning_refiner combination and into the
			dereplication that decides which bin owns each contig; annotate_bins would have run
			prokka on it; classify_bins would have asked taxator to place it; reassemble_bins
			would have recruited reads to it and reassembled it through SPAdes
		found while asking where the recall goes in refinement. On the CAMI2 GI run nothing went
			wrong, because CONCOCT's leftovers came to 69.8 MB and refinement drops any bin over
			20 MB - the right outcome reached for a reason that has nothing to do with what the
			file is, and only while the catch-all stays large. A tighter CONCOCT run leaves a
			smaller one, and a smaller one is refined as if it were a genome
		the exclusion now lives in io.seqio.fasta_files, which was already the shared answer to
			"what does a bin folder contain", with constants.is_unbinned_name as the predicate -
			matched on bin_label so it still holds once an extension is normalised (unbinned.fa
			-> unbinned.fasta) or a bin set is gzipped, each of which is a point at which a check
			against the filename would quietly start letting the file through
		fifteen tests, including one that pins that refinement's size filter still drops
			oversized bins - the fix could have been made by tightening that filter instead,
			which would have passed any test that only counted the bins at the end
		it also moved a measurement. "The size filter dropped 11 of CONCOCT's 100 bins holding
			46% of the set's sequence", recorded while tracking down where refinement's recall
			goes, was that filter counted with the catch-all among the bins; its 69.8 MB is
			most of the difference. With the catch-all excluded it is 10 of 99 bins and 41%,
			7 of them over the ceiling and the largest 131.8 MB
	Fixed - refining twice into the same output folder failed at the very end, having done the work
		--force cleaned the bins* working sets out of the output root but not out of work_files,
			which is where a finished run leaves them. The tidy-up at the end then moved the new
			run's sets in on top with os.replace, which overwrites a file and refuses a directory
			that already has anything in it, so the run raised FileExistsError after writing
			every final bin and plotting the figures - nothing in the log after "MOVING OVER
			TEMPORARY FILES", and the remaining sets left half-moved for the next --resume
		worse than it reads, because the move runs before the final bins are laid out: the
			metawrap_70_10_bins sitting in the folder afterwards is the previous run's, so a
			harness that counted it recorded a complete result for a run that never produced one
		one line further on, copytree onto metawrap_<c>_<x>_bins would have failed for the same
			reason. --force now also removes the previous final set and its .stats.tsv /
			.contigs.tsv, matched on shape (constants.is_refined_bins_dirname) rather than by
			name, because the directory is named for the thresholds it was cut at and a rerun at
			different -c/-x would otherwise leave two final sets with nothing to say which is
			this run's
		the bins* clean takes sidecars too. A binsC.stats.tsv from a 3-set run surviving into a
			2-set rerun is as misleading as the folder was, and it is what the stats tables and
			the plots are built from
		a rerun also kept presenting the previous run's input sets as its own. Refinement copies
			each input set back into the output folder under that folder's name, which is where
			anything downstream looks for "what went in", and it wrote the copy only when the
			destination did not already exist - so the copy was never refreshed. A refined/
			directory was found advertising a 188-bin MaxBin2 set, with a matching .stats.tsv,
			beside the results of refining the corrected 129-bin one; scoring the inputs from the
			output folder would have scored the superseded set and blamed refinement for the
			difference. --force now removes those copies too, skipping any whose path resolves to
			the input itself, since nothing stops -A pointing inside the output directory
		found by re-refining a benchmark arm in place after its binner was corrected, which is the
			ordinary reason to do this. Four tests, each of which was failing before one of the
			four fixes above: a plain rerun, a rerun leaving no bin set in the output root, a
			rerun whose input set changed, and a 3-set run re-run with 2 sets
	Fixed - --resume on bin_refinement deleted what it was resuming from, and still reported success
		the clean above ran whenever the output folder existed, which is as true of --resume as it
			is of --force, and --resume means the opposite: it is about to skip the steps whose
			checkpoints are set, so clearing their output leaves nothing to skip to. A resume of a
			completed run deleted the final bin set and then announced "BIN_REFINEMENT PIPELINE
			FINISHED SUCCESSFULLY" over an output folder that no longer had any bins in it
		the clean is now gated on a new _common.force(), the counterpart to the existing dry_run().
			Since the bin sets only move into work_files at the finalize step, the old behaviour
			destroyed something on every resume from "refine" onwards
	Fixed - the same two defects in reassemble_bins, which is the most expensive module to lose a run of
		the shuffle into work_files used the same bare os.replace onto directory destinations that
			a previous run had already filled, so a second reassembly into the same folder raised
			OSError: Directory not empty after every SPAdes assembly was finished
		it had no clean at all, only a warning - and reassembled_bins is added to rather than
			replaced. So a second run's candidate pool contained the first run's promoted winners,
			including its .orig.fa copies of input bins that may since have changed, and "the best
			version of each bin" was chosen across two runs. Nothing in the output said so
		--force now clears the previous reassembly (intermediates, work_files, the chosen-bins list
			and the reassembled_bins sidecars), leaving original_bins, the log and the provenance
			record alone - there would otherwise be nothing to show the first run had happened
		the two blocks are now _shuffle_intermediates and _clean_previous_run so they can be tested
			without running SPAdes, which is why neither was covered before. Five tests
	Fixed - a --resume that restarts a crashed step no longer trips over what the crash left behind
		gating the clean on --force exposed the other half of the same problem: a resumed run that
			has to redo a step arrives with that step's half-written output in place. Binning_refiner
			moving its result onto bins<AB>, the copytree that seeds binsM, and the dereplication
			writing binsO would each have refused or silently merged with it - and dereplicate writes
			into its output folder rather than replacing it, so a crashed attempt's bins would have
			survived into the final set
		the guard is now one helper, _common.fs_replace_dir: os.replace with the destination cleared
			first if it is a directory, skipped under --dry-run like the other fs_* helpers. It
			handles files too, which is what the tidy-up loops moving bin sets and their .stats.tsv
			sidecars in one pass need
	Fixed - metaBAT2 was given no seed, and its default means "pick a random one"
		metabat2's "--seed arg (=0)" documents 0 as a random seed, so the default binner - the
			baseline every other binner is ranked against - produced a different answer on every
			run, and a user re-running their own study got different MAGs with nothing to say why.
			SemiBin2, VAMB and COMEBin were all seeded when they went in because they train nets;
			metaBAT2 was missed because it does not look like a tool that needs one. It now gets
			the same fixed 1337
		found by three benchmark arms running metaBAT2 on a byte-identical depth table with a
			byte-identical command line, two producing 79 bins and the third 78. Nothing else about
			the runs differed, which left the seed as the only candidate
		those three arms are therefore three draws of one binner on one input, which is the only
			chance to measure what it cost and is gone now the seed is pinned: two of the three are
			identical to the contig (Rand index 1.00000), the third differs in 294 contigs, and the
			widest metric spread is 0.0042 on precision. The binner-set difference it was confounding
			is 0.0686 on F1, sixteen times that - so no recorded comparison changes
		CONCOCT's seed is now passed explicitly too, at its own default of 1 rather than at
			metaBAT2's value, so this cannot be the reason a CONCOCT result moves. Same argument as
			pinning CoverM's mapper: the knob exists ("0 gives a random seed") and a tool changing
			its default underneath a user changes their MAGs. MaxBin2 has no seed to pass, and
			salmon's only matters with bootstraps, which this pipeline does not ask for
	Fixed - AMBER scored one bin set twice and reported it as two arms
		the converted query file was named after the bin directory, and bin_refinement names its
			final directory for the thresholds it was cut at - so scoring two arms whose sets were
			both cut at "-c 70 -x 10" wrote metawrap_70_10_bins.binning twice, the second over the
			first, and handed AMBER the same file under both labels. It returned two identical rows
			to four decimal places for bin sets of 74 and 60 bins, with nothing in its output to say
			the queries were the same file
		named after the label instead, which is unique by construction because it is what tells the
			rows apart. Duplicate labels, and a label count that does not match the number of bin
			sets, are now errors rather than a quietly wrong table - AMBER matches labels to sets by
			position, so a mismatch relabels every row after the gap
	New - "metawrap2 cohort", so binning each sample separately is a pipeline and not a loop
		one assembly per sample, one coverage pass across all of them, binning and consolidation per
			sample, then one dereplication into a genome catalogue that the quantifier and the
			annotators see instead of any single sample's bins. The same sample sheet, manifest,
			checkpoints, run lock, resource budget, history and --resume as "run": it is the same
			planner with the assembly mode decided, not a second driver to keep correct
		this fixes a real break rather than adding a mode. "coassemble = false" already produced
			per-sample assemblies - and then pointed binning and quant_bins at
			ASSEMBLY/final_assembly.fasta, which that mode never writes, and reassembly at
			CLEAN_READS/ALL_READS_*, which is only concatenated when co-assembling. The path existed
			and could not run; a test now walks every step of an all-steps cohort plan and fails on
			any reference to a co-assembly-only path
		the default steps follow the mode: a cohort adds coverage and dereplicate, because they only
			mean anything once each sample has its own assembly, and leaves out reassemble_bins,
			because it is the most expensive step in the pipeline and here it would run once per
			sample. Naming steps explicitly still wins over both defaults
		quant_bins runs once against the catalogue with no -a. Near-identical MAGs recovered from
			several samples would otherwise divide one organism's reads between them, and there is no
			single assembly to quantify against in this mode
		each per-sample stage is one parallel group, so -j N moves N samples through a stage rather
			than one sample end to end - the thread and memory budget is divided by the driver, which
			is the only thing that knows how many steps are running
		mixing the modes is reported rather than obeyed: coverage and dereplicate are dropped from a
			co-assembly with a message saying why (one assembly, one bin set), and a sheet asking to
			co-assemble under "metawrap2 cohort" is overridden with a warning naming "run" as the way
			to honour it
		which of the two recovers more clean genomes per CPU-hour is still unmeasured - that is
			benchmark B5, which needed this driver to exist before it could be run
	New - "metawrap2 coverage --coverage fairy", a depth table with no alignment at all
		fairy sketches each sample's reads once and estimates every contig's depth in every sample
			from k-mer containment, writing the same columns
			jgi_summarize_bam_contig_depths writes - contigName, contigLen, totalAvgDepth, then a
			depth and a variance per sample. Verified against the installed tool before any of this
			was written, because a route that emits a nearly-right table is worse than one that
			cannot run: the split, the slices and every binner downstream are unchanged, and the
			flag is the only difference between the two routes
		it is minutes where aligning is hours, and the cost is resolution. fairy's -m 95 default
			resolves nothing below roughly 95% ANI, so reads from a strain that diverges from the
			contig it assembled into are credited to that contig or to nothing, and a sketch has no
			positions within a contig - so metaBAT2's intra-contig depth variance is filled from
			containment rather than measured per base
		"align" stays the default on measurement, not on caution. Fed to metaBAT2 on one CAMI2 GI
			sample, single-sample fairy scored AMBER F1 0.212 against minimap2's 0.452 - half the
			bins - for 0.012 cpu-hours against 1.368, and raising its ANI threshold made agreement
			with the aligners worse rather than better. Ranked on F1 per cpu-hour alone it wins by
			50x, which is the clearest case in this repo for a fidelity floor under that ratio
		what that run could not test is fairy's own claim, which is a multi-sample one - 98.5% of
			MAGs recovered across samples - and needs the cross-sample table the coverage module
			exists to build. That is benchmark B5's coverage arm; the default does not move first
		the sketches are kept rather than swept, unlike the BAMs. They are small and they do not
			depend on the catalogue, so a later run against a different catalogue reuses them, and
			a --resume picks them up by name
		an interleaved input is sketched as single-end. A sketch records which k-mers a sample
			contains, not which mate they came from, so deinterleaving would cost a pass over the
			reads to arrive at the same sketch
		its own environment (metawrap2-coverage-fairy, one static binary) rather than a line in
			binning's, so a user on the default route never waits for it to solve:
			"metawrap2 install-env coverage-fairy"
	New - "metawrap2 coverage", so a cohort's cross-sample depth costs N alignments and not N^2
		multi-sample binning wants every contig's depth in every sample, and the obvious way to get
			that maps all N samples against each of the N per-sample assemblies. Twenty samples is four
			hundred alignments for twenty depth tables. This concatenates the assemblies into one contig
			catalogue, maps each sample once against it, builds one table, and splits that table back
			into one slice per assembly for "binning --depth" - twenty alignments for the same twenty
			tables, each of which now carries every sample's coverage
		the split is the part with a way to go quietly wrong, so the naming is checked rather than
			trusted. Contig names collide across assemblies because every assembler numbers from one, so
			each contig is prefixed with its assembly's label and the prefix is what the split
			demultiplexes on. A contig name or a label containing the separator, and two assemblies
			sharing a label, are errors at the catalogue - where they can be reported - rather than a
			slice of plausible depths attributed to the wrong sample. --separator takes any character the
			names do not use; the default ~ is one SPAdes, MEGAHIT and Flye never emit
		the slices carry the original contig names, not the prefixed ones, because each is handed to a
			binning run over the original assembly. Prefixed names against an unprefixed assembly is
			exactly what "binning --depth" refuses, so leaving them in would turn a correct run into a
			hard stop one command later. A label matching no contig in the catalogue is an error too,
			not an empty table to hand a binner
		-a LABEL=path exists because the obvious cohort layout defeats labelling by filename: twenty
			samples of sample_N/final_assembly.fasta would all be labelled final_assembly
		the BAMs and the catalogue are swept once the tables are written (--keep-bams,
			--keep-catalogue), which matters more here than in binning: the catalogue is every assembly
			together and minimap2's index of it is larger again
		it shares binning's conda env rather than having one of its own, since it runs the same
			aligner, samtools and jgi_summarize_bam_contig_depths. install-env now resolves a module's
			yaml from the env it maps to instead of from the module name, and builds a shared env once
			per run - resolving by module name would have made "install-env --all" report no env file
			for coverage and return 1, which is the command the installation docs lead with
		whether this beats per-sample binning on accuracy per CPU-hour is unmeasured: benchmark B5
			(co-assembly vs single-sample vs multi-sample) is what decides that, and it needs the cohort
			driver this module is a stage of
	Added - "metawrap2 binning --depth", so cohort coverage can be N mappings and not N per assembly
		binning from a contig x sample depth table that already exists, aligning nothing and
			needing no reads. metaBAT2 and MaxBin2 only: CONCOCT builds its own coverage table and
			SemiBin2, VAMB and COMEBin read the BAM directory, so selecting one of those with
			--depth is refused by name rather than silently aligning anyway
		this is the piece multi-split coverage needs. Mapping every sample against each sample's
			own assembly is N^2 alignments; mapping each sample once against the concatenated
			catalogue and handing each sample's binning its slice of that table is N
		the table is checked against the assembly before anything runs, because the dangerous input
			is not a malformed table but a well-formed one built against different contigs - which is
			exactly what a wrongly sliced cohort table is. No overlap at all is an error; a partial
			overlap is reported from both sides, since contigs missing from the table are left
			unbinned while rows naming contigs the assembly does not have are the normal result of
			slicing a cohort catalogue. A file whose first three columns are not
			contigName/contigLen/totalAvgDepth, or that has no per-sample column, is refused too:
			metaBAT2 accepts both and bins on whatever the columns happen to mean
		the positional reads are now optional, so a run with neither reads nor --depth is caught in
			the module with that choice spelled out rather than by argparse
	New - "metawrap2 dereplicate", so a cohort's MAG count counts organisms and not bins
		binning each sample separately recovers an abundant organism once per sample, and nothing
			downstream knows the copies are copies: a count over the pooled folders counts bins, and
			a quantifier handed near-identical genomes splits one organism's reads between them, so
			every copy looks rarer than the organism is. The module takes any number of bin folders
			with a repeated -b and returns one representative per cluster in dereplicated_genomes/,
			symlinked unless --copy
		identity comes from skani (triangle -E), which sketches rather than aligns and so scales to
			the pairs a cohort produces; the clustering and the choice of representative are Python,
			where they are unit-tested rather than inferred from a tool's behaviour. Its own env,
			"metawrap2 install-env dereplicate", rather than a share of the quant_bins-coverm env,
			which contains skani today only because CoverM depends on it
		--ani defaults to 95, the threshold large MAG catalogues use for a species. Recorded as a
			convention that makes the count comparable with a published one, not as a measurement of
			ours; --ani 99 keeps strains apart. A pair must also share --min-aligned-fraction of a
			genome, 10 by default and deliberately permissive, because 99% identity over 5% of two
			genomes is a shared prophage rather than one organism. The larger of skani's two aligned
			fractions decides, since a half-complete bin aligns almost fully to its complete
			neighbour while the neighbour aligns only half back, and taking the smaller would refuse
			to collapse exactly the duplicates this exists to collapse
		the representative is the best-scoring member, read from the <bins>.stats.tsv bin_refinement
			already leaves beside each folder, not the largest: a scored genome beats an unscored one
			however large, because size says a bin is big and not that it is one organism, and a 6 Mb
			chimera must not stand in for the clean 3 Mb copy. Scores are matched per folder, so two
			samples' bin_001 cannot take each other's row. With no table anywhere the module says so,
			names the consequence and falls back to size
		clustering is single linkage, so it chains: a-b and b-c at 96% merge all three even when a
			and c are not. Accepted because it never splits a genuine duplicate, which is the failure
			dereplication exists to prevent, and because clusters.tsv records each member's
			ani_to_representative - a member pulled in by chaining has none, which is what an
			over-merged cluster looks like from outside. The property is pinned in a test so a switch
			to complete linkage has to change it deliberately
		which ANI, which aligned fraction, which skani preset and which linkage are the right
			defaults are unmeasured here: there is no dereplicator comparison yet, so these are
			inherited conventions and are labelled as such in the source
	Fixed - a study sheet selecting a learned binner had its bins dropped before refinement
		the driver's list of binners was a literal ("metabat2", "maxbin2", "concoct") written before
			SemiBin2, VAMB and COMEBin existed, so binning ran them and refinement never saw them.
			It now reads the binning module's own BINNERS, which is the only list of them
		the quiet case was "--metabat2 --semibin2", which refined metaBAT2 alone. The loud one was
			"--semibin2" by itself: with nothing recognised the driver fell through to the default
			pair and pointed refinement at two directories no binner had created
		refinement is now handed its bin sets as repeated --bins rather than -A/-B/-C, because the
			letters stop at F and zipping three of them against six selected binners dropped the
			rest without saying so. --bins is the form bin_refinement documents for a caller that
			generates its own arguments, which is what the driver is
	Changed - "metawrap2 run" bins with metaBAT2 and MaxBin2 by default, not three binners
		CONCOCT comes out of the default set on measured cost. On cami2_gi it was 192 of the 213
			cpu-hours a three-binner run spent binning, and the pair without it produced 1.03 clean
			MAGs per cpu-hour against 0.35 - where clean means passing GUNC, so the 14 extra clean
			MAGs the third binner finds are counted in its favour and it still loses by 3x
		it is a default, not a capability: --concoct still selects it, bin_refinement still takes
			three bin sets, and the three-binner arm is still in benchmarks/configurations.toml with
			its numbers. What changed is only what runs when the sheet does not say
		recorded with the caveat rather than as a clean win, because the arm that wins on cost is
			also the dirtiest of the three consolidated sets measured (18.9% chimeric against 14.9%
			and 6.7%), and MaxBin2 alone is the dirtiest input in the table at 38.8%. That is a
			throughput decision taken knowingly, which is why gunc_pass_fraction is now a declared
			metric of the comparison and why the second slot is treated as unsettled
		the pair is asserted in tests against both the code constant and the variant the benchmark
			marks as the incumbent, so the two records of one decision cannot drift apart - which
			has happened before, on refinement_completeness, where the marked incumbent was a row
			no module default matched
	Added - "benchmark score" can run GUNC, so a binner comparison can be ranked on chimerism
		asking for gunc_pass_fraction - by a comparison declaring it or by --metric - now runs GUNC
			per bin set into <bin set>.gunc, beside CheckM's <bin set>.checkm, at score time and
			outside the measured run for the same reason the other assessors are
		which closes the gap that decided the CONCOCT question by hand: the binning module does not
			run GUNC, bin_refinement runs it once on the set it returns, so a comparison of binners
			had no chimerism column at all and three arms' rates had to be measured with a script
			outside the harness. The numbers that overturned a conclusion were not reproducible by
			anyone running the benchmark
		it is the one assessor whose cost is worth stating before anyone turns it on: 3.2 to 5.7
			CPU-hours per bin set at 48 threads on the arms measured here, dominated by diamond. A
			set whose report is already present is skipped unless --reassess, and a missing GUNC
			database is reported per variant rather than discovered an hour into a diamond run
		CheckM and GUNC are now skipped per assessor rather than per bin set, which the old
			one-file-stands-for-all check would have got wrong the moment a second per-set assessor
			existed: every set that had been through CheckM would have been skipped before GUNC ran
		the binner_set comparison declares the metric, so the chimera rates are part of the
			comparison rather than a footnote to it. The cost is in the config beside it, because a
			comparison that quietly costs a hundred cpu-hours to score is not a cheap half
	Fixed - AMBER was skipped on any variant whose bins had already been through CheckM
		the "already assessed" test was one CheckM table per bin set, standing in for every
			assessor, and AMBER writes one table per variant rather than one per set - so scoring a
			comparison for AMBER alone, on bins that had been through CheckM earlier, found nothing
			outstanding and ran nothing. AMBER is now asked about on its own output
	Fixed - the ranker added fractions together, so the variant measured on the most samples won
		time_to_performance summed every metric across a dataset's samples, which is right for
			bins recovered and CPU-hours spent and wrong for anything already normalised. Ten
			samples at 0.9 purity came to 9.0, and the inflation was not the real problem: two
			variants that scored identically were then separated by how many samples each had
			been measured on, with the ten-sample one ranking first at 9.0 against 1.8
		metrics whose declared unit is a fraction or an index are now averaged over the samples
			that recorded them, and the cost side is still summed, because ten samples do cost
			ten samples' CPU-hours. The mean is unweighted - a five-bin sample counts as much as
			a two-hundred-bin one - so the table says when it took one, and Ratio carries the
			sample count that went into the figure
		GUNC's pass fraction is recorded per bin set like the CheckM and AMBER metrics, rather
			than under one name for every report found. GUNC names the database in its filename
			and never the bins, so the directory is the evidence: the final set's report goes to
			work_files/gunc and anything else is suffixed with its path. No run produced two
			reports, so nothing recorded so far changes
	Added - bin_refinement reports what its size filter took
		the per-bin "Skipping ..." lines never added up to a figure, so a filter that removes
			41% of a bin set's sequence says nothing a user would read. Locating that drop cost
			a separate AMBER run per refinement stage
		the two ends are reported apart because they are different diagnoses: over the 20 MB
			ceiling is a catch-all bin that could never have become a MAG under any threshold,
			so losing it costs only the recall figure; under the 50 kb floor is a fragment,
			which another binner in the set might have grown into something
	Fixed - MaxBin2 was binning on depths that jgi_summarize_bam_contig_depths invented
		"--noIntraDepthVariance" reads as a formatting flag - MaxBin2 wants one depth per contig
			per sample and has no use for the variance columns - but in jgi 2.18 that code path
			does not drop columns, it reports numbers that are not depths. It is gone, and
			MaxBin2's per-sample abundance files are now split out of the same table metaBAT2
			reads, taking the mean columns and skipping the variances next to them
		measured by running the same two BAMs through the same tool with and without the flag.
			On a 60-contig probe it zeroed 59 of the 60 contigs and gave the 60th a depth of
			7867 where the real per-sample depths were 0.267 and 0.453 - and 7867 did not
			reproduce, coming back 7874 on an identical rerun, so the value is uninitialized
			memory. Handed a single BAM it returns 0 for everything
		on the real 210,632-contig CAMI2 GI assembly with three samples it reported no coverage
			for 57% of the contigs that have coverage in two of the three samples (37,453 of
			65,375 and 32,557 of 56,369), gave ~25,000 contigs with no reads a coverage anyway,
			and overstated others by up to 1.5e9x. In an EM over abundance a contig at depth
			10^9 is not noise, it is the signal
		nothing failed, which is why this needed finding on purpose: a table of wrong depths is
			a well-formed abundance file, and MaxBin2 bins on composition too, so it kept
			returning plausible bins. Every multi-sample MaxBin2 result produced before this is
			affected, including the metabat2_maxbin2 and metabat2_maxbin2_concoct arms of the
			binner_set comparison, which have to be re-run before they can settle anything
		the fix also removes a jgi pass. jgi reads every BAM start to finish and is the
			expensive part of the coverage stage; it ran once per binner for two tables that
			were always meant to hold the same numbers. The table is now built once, in its own
			checkpointed stage, so a --resume that adds MaxBin2 to a finished metaBAT2 run does
			not rebuild it, and neither binner owns a file the other depends on
		nine tests, including one that asserts a sample whose BAM is named "var.bam" is not
			mistaken for a variance column, and one that runs metaBAT2 alone and then resumes
			with MaxBin2 added, which has to realign and rebuild the table rather than bin on
			one built from a different read set
		what it was costing, measured by re-binning the CAMI2 GI assembly on a real table and
			scoring both sets in one AMBER invocation: precision 0.6485 -> 0.8016, ARI 0.7079 ->
			0.8747, F1 0.4228 -> 0.4598. The fixed run returns 129 bins over 349.8 Mbp where the
			buggy one returned 188 over 384.0, and recall still goes up (0.3137 -> 0.3223) - so
			the 59 bins and 34 Mbp that went were not genomes the fix lost, they were sequence
			the invented depths were putting in the wrong bin
	Fixed - reassemble_bins was recruiting reads that are not reads, and scoring unknowns as perfect
		found while checking whether reassemble_bins could take --aligner too. Neither of its two
			read filters had a test, which is how three defects in them survived; they have eight
			now, and each one dies under the fix reverted
		"did this record align" came off the presence of an NM:i: tag in both filters, which is a
			claim about bwa rather than about SAM. It now comes off the FLAG's unmapped bit. The
			proxy was load-bearing and not obviously wrong: an unmapped mate is not necessarily
			parked at RNAME "*", because the spec lets it be placed at its mate's reference and
			bwa does exactly that, so the reference name alone cannot tell the two apart
		neither filter excluded secondary or supplementary alignments, and the long-read one is
			where that was reaching SPAdes: "minimap2 -ax map-ont" emits secondary alignments by
			default, writes their SEQ as "*", and sets NM on them, so the NM test let each one
			through and it became a one-base read in the FASTQ a bin was reassembled from. A long
			read crossing a repeat produces several
		in the short-read filter the same omission lost whole pairs rather than adding junk ones:
			a supplementary record carries the first-mate bit, so it displaced the real forward
			mate and was then yielded against the next reverse mate - two different reads, paired,
			and recruited on where neither of them aligned
		a missing NM scored as 0, which is a *perfect* alignment, so a record whose edit distance
			nobody wrote down was sorted into the strict set on the strength of it. An unknown is
			now permissive-only, and the mismatch sum counts just the mates that aligned, so an
			unmapped mate no longer contributes a flawless 0 to a half-aligned pair
	Fixed - the refinement_completeness comparison marked a threshold nothing ships as its incumbent
		"default = true" sat on c50_x10 while bin_refinement's DEFAULT_MIN_COMPLETION is 70,
			so "benchmark list" reported "-c 50 -x 10 <- current default" and a results
			table would have put the incumbent marker on a row no user's run produces.
			That marker exists precisely so a proposed default is read next to the one it
			would replace, which it cannot do while naming the wrong one
		c70_x10 now carries it, and c50_x10 is written up as what it is: MIMAG's
			medium-quality floor rather than the 70 metaWRAP used, and the looser of the two
	Fixed - a missing opt-in conda env is reported before the work instead of after it
		env_for had always checked that a module's own env exists, so a missing one was
			reported in the first second of a run. Opt-in envs were read straight out of
			OPTIONAL_ENVS with no such check, so the first sign of trouble was mamba
			complaining about a missing prefix at whatever point in the pipeline the tool
			was reached - which for a learned binner is after every read has been aligned,
			and for GUNC is after CheckM1 and consolidation have both finished
		optional_env_for does the same check for every opt-in env - the three learned
			binners, CheckM2, GUNC, Bakta, GTDB-Tk and the alternative quantifiers - and
			names the install-env command for the env it wants rather than the prefix path
			mamba would have printed. With use_conda_envs = false it short-circuits, since
			the tool is then expected on PATH and there is no env to miss
		the message names what asked for the env, and the caller passes that rather than it
			being derived from the env's key, because the obvious derivation of "--" + key
			is wrong for half of them: GUNC runs by default and is turned *off* with
			--skip-gunc, and CoverM is selected as --quantifier coverm. A derived message
			would have told users to pass --gunc and --coverm, neither of which exists. A
			test walks every call site's phrase and checks each flag in it against the
			parser that would have to accept it
		binning resolves all of its learned binners' envs in main(), before the alignment
			stage, rather than at the point each binner runs. That placement is the whole
			point of the fix: checking at the point of use would still fail fast relative
			to nothing, but it would do so an hour into the run
	Fixed - a missing env manager is reported before the work instead of at the first tool
		running anything inside a conda env needs two things to hold: the env has to exist,
			and the manager that enters it has to be findable. Only the first was checked
			up front. The second was checked where the argv is built, which is not the
			start of a run but the first step that happens to need an env
		bin_refinement runs Binning_refiner in the host interpreter, so with mamba off PATH
			a 3-binner refinement built all four combinations - seven minutes of real work
			on a CAMI2 arm - and then printed "mamba is not on PATH" at CheckM. Found that
			way, while measuring what refinement costs each binner_set arm
		env_for and optional_env_for both check it now, so the message arrives in the first
			second for a module's own env and before the work for an opt-in one. The
			message itself was already right; the only thing wrong with it was when the
			user got to read it. Under --dry-run it is skipped, since nothing is executed
	Fixed - the hybrid assembly selects unassembled reads by the unmapped flag, not by a bwa tag
		the reads metaSPAdes left behind were the records with no "NM:i:" tag, which is a claim
			about one aligner rather than about SAM: bwa writes an edit distance on every
			aligned record, so its absence stood in for "did not align". minimap2 is
			MetaWrap2's measured default aligner everywhere else, and an aligner that omits
			the tag would have made every mapped read look unmapped - failing by handing
			MEGAHIT the entire read set rather than by stopping
		it reads FLAG 0x4 now, and the output is byte-identical on real bwa data: 33,332,411
			records from a CAMI2 sample, zero records classified differently in either
			direction. So this changes no existing result; it stops the selection depending
			on which aligner produced the stream
		the unfiltered mode also drops secondary and supplementary records (FLAG 0x100/0x800),
			which are further alignments of a read already emitted rather than further
			reads, and a header line with eleven or more tab-separated fields - an @CO
			comment - is no longer emitted as a read, which it was
		reading the flag costs 650 against 1041 MB per CPU-second, because the tag test could
			reject a record on a substring search without splitting it. Spent knowingly:
			the aligner upstream produces SAM at 26 MB/s (bwa) to 146 MB/s (minimap2) on
			this hardware, so the converter keeps over 4x headroom and is not what the pipe
			waits on. "samtools fastq -f 4" says the same thing and was measured too - 711
			MB per CPU-second, and it would put samtools in an env with no other use for it
	Changed - the unix-socket path limit is handled for every tool that takes a temp directory
		the shortening that CheckM1 needed moved from metawrap2.checkm to
			metawrap2.utils.short_tmpdir, because SemiBin2 has the same constraint for the
			same reason: --tmpdir there exports TMPDIR, and anything that then binds a
			multiprocessing listener socket inside it has 107 characters to work with
	Fixed - CheckM1 no longer dies with a bare "EOFError" when the output path is deep
		"checkm lineage_wf --tmpdir X" makes CheckM set tempfile.tempdir to X for the whole
			process, and the first thing it does is mp.Manager().dict(), whose listener
			socket is therefore bound inside X. Linux allows a unix-socket path 107
			characters; MetaWrap2 passes "<bin set>.tmp", so a 90-character output path left
			no room, bind() failed in the Manager's child, and the parent raised EOFError
			with nothing in it about paths. It cost a whole benchmark comparison its
			bins_near_complete and bins_high_quality metrics, on every variant
		the temp directory still lives beside the bins - CheckM's tree stage writes GBs and
			they belong on the filesystem the caller chose, not on whatever /tmp is - but
			when that path is too long CheckM is handed a short symlink to it instead.
			bind() resolves the symlink, so the socket name is short and the bytes still
			land where they were going. The link is named from a hash of the real path, so
			a --resume reuses it rather than leaking one per attempt
	Changed - "metawrap2 benchmark score --checkm2"
		"score --checkm2" scores bin quality with CheckM2 rather than CheckM1. The assessor
			had supported CheckM2 all along and nothing on the command line could ask for
			it, which is worth having for its own sake: CheckM1's pplacer stage wants
			roughly 40 GB per thread, and CheckM2 predicts completeness from a neural net
			and needs a fraction of that. AMBER metrics are unaffected either way - they
			score against a gold standard, not against marker genes
	Changed - bowtie2 retired; blobology maps with minimap2 and shares binning's aligner registry
		bowtie2 was blobology's only aligner and the last module using it. blobology maps
			reads back for the same reason binning does - per-contig abundance, here for the
			plot's y-axis - so the aligner benchmark already answered it: bowtie2 cost what
			bwa cost (7.44 cpu-hours, 2.58 of that building the index alone) and agreed with
			bwa less closely than minimap2 did (Pearson 0.825). No input makes it the right
			answer, so it is dropped rather than demoted
		the registry moved to metawrap2.aligners, shared by binning and blobology. Two
			modules asking the same question had two answers that could drift; now there is
			one, and blobology gained --aligner (minimap2 default, bwa and bwa-mem2 offered)
			for free. Its env carries all three, so --aligner needs no second install
		blobology does not sort its BAM, deliberately: gc_cov_annotate reads it with a plain
			samtools view and sums aligned span per contig, so record order is irrelevant
		its per-sample BAM is no longer named <sample>.bowtie2.bam. An output filename should
			not carry the name of a tool the user can now change with a flag
		bowtie2 stays in read_qc, where bmtagger genuinely needs it for host removal
	Changed - no module writes a SAM to disk any more, and binning discards its BAMs
		binning already piped its aligner into samtools sort; assembly and blobology did not.
			blobology was the worse of the two - it wrote a full uncompressed SAM per sample,
			converted it to BAM, and never deleted the SAM, so a multi-sample blobplot left
			every one of them behind permanently. assembly's hybrid path wrote the aligner's
			whole SAM, filtered it into a second SAM, converted that, then deleted both:
			roughly 25 GB of text at peak to produce one FASTQ. Both now stream
		binning deletes the per-sample BAMs, their .bai indexes and the aligner's index of
			the assembly once every requested binner has taken its depth table, since nothing
			downstream reads them - 0.6 GB of 1.0 GB on one real sample, and ~5 GB per sample
			of BAM on a cohort, against the few MB of TSV that is all a binner wanted from it.
			The aligner index is the easy one to miss: minimap2's .mmi for a 154 MB assembly
			is 479 MB, larger than the BAM next to it. --keep-bams keeps them
			deleting them clears the align_reads checkpoint, so a later --resume that adds a
			binner realigns instead of resuming into a stage whose inputs are gone
		every new pipe carries set -o pipefail. samtools view and the FASTQ converter both
			exit 0 on a truncated stream, so without it an aligner that died halfway would
			read as a clean run - a blobplot drawn from partial coverage, or a hybrid
			assembly built from a slice of the unassembled reads, with nothing in the log
	Changed - binning measures coverage with minimap2 by default (was bwa), --aligner to choose
		none of the three binners reads an alignment. They read coverage - metaBAT2 and MaxBin2
			through jgi_summarize_bam_contig_depths, CONCOCT through concoct_coverage_table.py -
			so the BAM is a temporary file on the way to a depth vector and the aligner is an
			implementation detail of a coverage estimator. That is the question that was measured:
			not which aligner maps best, but which route to the depth vector is cheapest for depth
			the binners treat the same way
		one CAMI2 GI sample mapped back to a 210,632-contig assembly, 32 threads, index and map
			timed separately because an index is built once per assembly and a map runs once per
			sample. Agreement is against bwa, over the contigs binning actually reads (>=1000 bp):
				              Pearson  Spearman  index CPU-h  per-sample  total CPU-h  peak RSS
				minimap2       0.9461    0.9841      0.009        1.359       1.368      6.7 GB
				bwa            1.0000    1.0000      0.223        7.551       7.774      9.8 GB
				bwa-mem2       1.0000    1.0000      0.075        5.123       5.199     23.6 GB
				bowtie2        0.8247    0.9751      2.581        4.856       7.437      6.8 GB
		minimap2 is deliberately not the closest to bwa here - it ranks contigs almost identically
			(Spearman 0.984) but differs on magnitude (Pearson 0.946, L1 0.183). Agreement with
			bwa is not the objective, though; bwa is the incumbent, not the truth. So the question
			was pushed through to the bins, which is the only place a depth difference can be
			shown to matter. Same metabat2, same flags, AMBER against CAMI2's own
			contig-to-genome truth:
				              F1 (bp)  recall  precision   ARI (bp)
				minimap2       0.4519   0.3118   0.8204     0.0609
				bwa            0.4493   0.3103   0.8133     0.0607
			minimap2 comes out fractionally ahead on all four. The differences are noise and the
				honest reading is a tie - a tie for a fifth of the CPU, with no trade-off left to
				adjudicate, which is rarer than it sounds
		"--aligner bwa" restores the previous behaviour exactly. "--aligner bwa-mem2" is offered
			because it is bit-identical to bwa on the depth vector (Pearson 1.0000, L1 0.000, the
			same 22,929 non-zero contigs) at 1.5x the speed - not the advertised 2-3x - but it
			wants 23.6 GB of RAM against bwa's 9.8 and a 5.3 GB index against 1.7 GB. That is a
			trade worth opting into and a poor one to impose
		bowtie2 is dropped rather than demoted. It was listed as a binning dependency and the
			module never invoked it; measured, it costs what bwa costs, spends 2.58 CPU-hours of
			that on index building alone, and agrees with bwa less closely than minimap2 does
		the caveat that keeps this honest: one sample against CAMI2's pooled gold-standard
			assembly, which is far less fragmented than a real metaSPAdes assembly, and
			fragmentation is exactly what separates aligners. Worth re-measuring there
		two cheaper routes were measured and rejected, both instructive. "strobealign --aemb"
			skips the BAM and writes per-contig depth directly, 21x cheaper than bwa - but
			per-contig depth is all it can write, and the intra-contig depth variance metaBAT2
			uses as a binning signal needs per-base coverage, which is exactly what --aemb
			declines to compute. "fairy" does not align at all, sketching k-mers instead, and is
			648x cheaper - and scored highest of everything on raw F1 per CPU-hour, which is what
			a decision rule of accuracy-per-cost says to pick. It is still the wrong answer: both
			routes are per-contig only, and CONCOCT cuts contigs into 10 kb fragments and needs
			per-fragment coverage out of an indexed BAM, so neither can feed it at all. A ratio
			needs a fidelity floor under it or it will eventually recommend measuring nothing
		fairy's --minimum-ani default of 95 was checked against the same suspicion that moved
			sylph's, and the suspicion was wrong: agreement with bwa degrades monotonically as the
			threshold rises (Pearson 0.237, 0.223, 0.194 at 95/97/99), the opposite of sylph's
			behaviour, so fairy's own default is the right one. Recorded because a refuted
			hypothesis is worth as much here as a confirmed one
	Fixed - a crashed aligner no longer reads as a successful run
		binning now pipes the aligner straight into "samtools sort" instead of writing the SAM
			out and deleting it a minute later, which was ~25 GB per sample of uncompressed text
			and the module's largest transient footprint
		the pipe needs "set -o pipefail": bash reports only the last command's status, and
			samtools sort fed a truncated stream still exits 0 and still writes a BAM. Without it
			an aligner that died of OOM produced "BINNING PIPELINE SUCCESSFULLY FINISHED!!!" and
			an empty coverage table. There is a test that fails if the flag is removed
		reassemble_bins had the same defect on its two alignment pipes, where the read-splitter
			on the right-hand side exits 0 on a truncated stream - so a half-finished alignment
			would have reassembled every bin from a silently truncated read subset
	Changed - read_qc trims with fastp by default (was Trim Galore)
		measured over all 10 CAMI2 GI samples (333M read pairs each way, one 96-thread budget):
			fastp kept marginally more reads (+0.03%), bases (+0.83%) and Q30 bases (+0.40%) for
			5.8% fewer CPU-hours and 29% less wall time. It wins on both halves of the ratio, so
			no trade-off had to be adjudicated - the quality difference is small enough to call a
			tie and the cost decides
		"--trimmer trim-galore" restores the previous behaviour and is fully supported. It is
			the better choice on one axis: it peaks at 0.1 GB of memory where fastp takes 1.3 GB
		no claim is made about MAG yield. A trimming difference this small is unlikely to
			survive assembly and binning, and unlikely is not measured
	Unchanged, now on evidence - assembly still defaults to MEGAHIT
		measured on three CAMI2 GI samples co-assembled (50M read pairs, one 96-thread budget):
			metaSPAdes returned a 38% longer N50 (12,874 bp vs 9,325) for 3.2x the CPU-hours
			(245 vs 77) and 9.7x the peak memory (140 GB vs 15 GB), and assembled 14% fewer
			bases in total (197 Mbp vs 228 Mbp)
		so MEGAHIT keeps the default on the ratio (120.6 bp of N50 per CPU-hour against 52.6),
			and on the plainer argument that 140 GB of resident memory is more than an ordinary
			workstation has, and a default that runs out of memory is not a default
		"--metaspades" is unchanged and is the right flag when contiguity is the point; with
			"--megahit" as well it still runs the hybrid pipeline
		no claim is made about MAG yield: binning was not run on the two assemblies. Unlike the
			trimmer's 0.8%, a 38% N50 difference plausibly would survive binning - plausibly is
			still not measured
	New - GUNC scores the final bins for chimerism
		bin_refinement runs GUNC on the refined set and adds gunc_css/gunc_pass to its
			.stats.tsv. A bin assembled from halves of two genomes has a complete and
			uncontaminated-looking marker set, so CheckM calls it good; GUNC maps genes against a
			reference taxonomy and asks whether they agree about where the bin sits
		bins are annotated, never dropped - the call belongs to whoever reads the table, and a
			bin GUNC could not assess is left blank rather than recorded as passing
		runs by default; --skip-gunc opts out, along with its 6.7 GB database
			("metawrap2 install-db gunc", "metawrap2 install-env bin_refinement-gunc")
	New - "metawrap2 install-db checkm2" installs CheckM2's database
		it was the one database the installer knew nothing about: --checkm2 told the user to run
			"checkm2 database --download" themselves and to point CHECKM2DB at the result. It is
			now a catalog entry like the others, fetched and recorded with a name rather than by
			hand
		the download is left to CheckM2 itself, because the file sits on a versioned Zenodo record
			that CheckM2 pins - a URL of our own goes stale the next time CheckM2 is upgraded
		the recorded path is passed to "checkm2 predict" as --database_path, so a run does not
			depend on what state the conda env was left in. With no path configured, CheckM2 still
			falls back to its own, which is what a copy installed by hand relies on
		it is not CheckM1's database, and having one does nothing for the other - the two entries
			say so, since both are commonly called "the CheckM database"
	New - "quant_bins --quantifier coverm", a second way to ask what a bin's abundance is
		CoverM works in genome mode: it maps each sample's reads against the bins themselves, so
			there is no pseudo-transcriptome to index and no per-contig count to collapse. One
			invocation covers every sample, since --coupled takes the pairs repeatedly and returns
			the whole genome x sample table at once
		the numbers are not interchangeable with salmon's, which is why this is a flag and not a
			substitution: salmon's cell is a length-weighted median contig coverage, CoverM's is
			relative abundance as a percentage of the sample, so a CoverM row sums to at most 100
			and a salmon row has no such ceiling. Both write bin_abundance_table.tsv and the same
			heatmap, and everything downstream is unchanged - but a table from one must not be
			compared against a table from the other
		which quantifier is the default is settled by measurement, not by which arrived first.
			See the sylph entry below for the three-way comparison and the scoring that made it
			possible
		the mapper is pinned to strobealign rather than left to CoverM. It is CoverM's own 0.8
			default, but it was not always, and a mapper swapping itself out underneath a user
			changes the numbers and not just the runtime
		--min-covered-fraction stays at CoverM's 10%, because a handful of reads landing on a
			conserved gene is not evidence that a genome is present
		the bins are named to CoverM in a list file instead of with --genome-fasta-directory and
			-x. A single extension has to be guessed at otherwise, a wrong guess yields an empty
			genome set rather than an error, and a folder holding both .fasta and .fasta.gz bins
			cannot be expressed at all
		CoverM has its own opt-in env, because it brings a mapper and an ANI stack the salmon env
			has no use for: "metawrap2 install-env quant_bins-coverm", once
	New - "quant_bins --quantifier sylph", abundance without alignment
		sylph (Shaw & Yu, Nat Biotechnol 2025) sketches the bins and the reads into k-mer sketches
			and profiles one against the other, so nothing is aligned and nothing is indexed in the
			mapping sense. The cost of profiling therefore barely grows with sequencing depth,
			which is the axis on which a read aligner has to pay for every extra read
		its numbers are on the same scale as CoverM's and not as salmon's: Sequence_abundance is a
			percentage of the sample. Taxonomic_abundance, sylph's other column, divides out genome
			length to give a fraction of organisms - a different and equally valid number, but not
			one salmon's coverage or a read-to-genome truth can be compared against, so the table
			is filled from Sequence_abundance
		a bin sylph does not detect is a zero in the table, and that zero means something narrower
			than CoverM's: "not detected above --min-ani", where CoverM's means "mapped, but under
			--min-covered-fraction of the genome was covered". sylph omits an undetected genome
			from its output entirely, so the table is pivoted against the genome list rather than
			against the profile, and every bin appears whether it was detected or not
		the genome list CoverM is given is the same file sylph's --gl takes, and the read pairing
			is the same pairing, so all three quantifiers see one set of bins and one set of samples
		sylph ships in the module's own env alongside salmon, because it is the default and a
			default that needs a second install command is not a default
	Changed - sylph replaces salmon as the default quantifier, measured not assumed
		76 bins from the CAMI2 GI gold-standard assembly, quantified across three samples against
			the read-level truth below, 32 threads each:
				                Spearman  Pearson    L1   CPU-hours  peak RSS  Spearman/CPU-hour
				sylph             0.868    0.811   0.555     0.19      1.3 GB        4.67
				salmon            0.827    0.959   0.331     1.08      7.4 GB        0.77
				CoverM            0.803    0.952   0.419     2.37     11.6 GB        0.34
		sylph orders the bins better than either aligner while costing a sixth of what salmon does
			and a twelfth of CoverM, so it wins on accuracy per CPU-hour by a wide margin and on
			rank accuracy outright - which is the metric an abundance table is usually read for
		what it does not win is magnitude. salmon's estimates correlate far better linearly and sit
			closer as a composition, so "--quantifier salmon" is the right choice when the numbers
			themselves are the point - a ratio between two bins rather than an ordering. It stays in
			the same env, so switching costs no install
		CoverM is now the odd one out: slowest, hungriest, and no more accurate than salmon. It stays
			available, and stays opt-in
	Changed - sylph's --minimum-ani is now a flag, --min-ani, and defaults to 99 rather than 95
		this was the setting that moved the numbers most, and sylph's own default was wrong for this
			job. At 95, four bins whose genomes are absent from a sample entirely took 55% of the
			abundance reported for it - relatives close enough to match at what is a species-level
			threshold applied to a strain-level question
				ANI    Spearman  Pearson    L1   misattributed mass
				 95      0.758    0.678   0.832       55%
				 97      0.823    0.744   0.728       44%
				 99      0.868    0.811   0.555       25%   <- default
				 99.5    0.857    0.800   0.536       25%
				 99.9    0.696    0.840   0.448       30%   (13 of 29 present bins missed entirely)
		99 is the turning point: past it the threshold discards bins that really are there and rank
			accuracy collapses even as the surviving estimates sharpen
		it is a flag rather than a constant because these bins came off a pooled gold-standard
			assembly and are near-complete; bins off a fragmented real assembly match their own
			genome less tightly, so a strict floor could cost more there than it did here
		below 95 the module warns. sylph itself says a lower value "will give inaccurate results" -
			its containment statistics are calibrated for same-species comparison, so going lower
			changes what the number means rather than how sensitive it is
		sylph's --estimate-unknown was measured too and changed nothing at any threshold, so it is
			not wired up
	New - "benchmark" scores abundance tables, so the quantifiers can be compared at all
		metawrap2.benchmark.abundance holds a bins x samples truth built from CAMI2's own
			reads/reads_mapping.tsv.gz, which says which genome every read in a sample came from.
			That is an abundance per genome; turning it into an abundance per bin is the step that
			needed thought
		a bin is expected to account for its genome's read fraction times its own share of that
			genome's assembly, because reads from the parts of the genome that did not make it
			into the bin cannot be attributed to the bin by any quantifier however good. Dropping
			the second factor scores a 60%-complete bin as an underestimate and marks the
			quantifier down for the binner's incompleteness
		the metrics are scale-free, because the three quantifiers report three different units and
			a metric that was not would rank them by which unit they happen to use: Spearman and
			Pearson correlation across bins within a sample, averaged over samples, plus an L1
			distance between the two compositions after both are renormalised to sum to 1.
			Spearman is the one to lean on - it asks whether the bins were put in the right order,
			which is what an abundance table is read for
		samples are averaged, never pooled. Pooling puts a deep sample's bins and a shallow one's
			on one axis, where the correlation measures the difference between the samples
		a bin whose genome is absent from a sample expects zero, and that is a real expectation:
			reporting a genome that is not there is an error worth counting
	Fixed
		scoring a binning comparison against CAMI2 could not have worked at all: the contig-to-genome
			truth was keyed on the mapping's anonymous_contig_id, and CAMI2's own gold-standard
			assembly does not use those names - it names a contig after the region it was cut from
			and leaves the pieces of that name in the mapping's contig_id, start_position and
			end_position columns. On the gastrointestinal challenge not one of 210,618 anonymous ids
			matched the 210,633-contig assembly, so every comparison would have died with "yielded
			no contig-to-genome pairs" and the dataset would have looked as though it disagreed with
			itself. The name the assembly uses is now reconstructed where the anonymous id does not
			match (210,618 of 210,618), and the anonymous id is still preferred where it does, so a
			dataset that uses them is unaffected
		a benchmark on CAMI2 would have measured the wrong file: reads/ holds the 10 GB
			anonymous_reads.fq and a 3 MB subsample_0.fq debugging extract under the same sample
			directory, so a suffix scan found both and the second overwrote the first. The
			dataset's declared reads_glob is now used (it was parsed and ignored), and two files
			resolving to one sample label is a hard failure rather than a silent overwrite
		a run launched without conda on PATH - as nohup, cron and batch schedulers do, since
			they do not read ~/.bashrc - died inside subprocess with "FileNotFoundError: mamba",
			which reads as the tool being missing rather than the thing meant to find it. It now
			stops when the command is built, naming both ways out, and "benchmark run" checks once
			up front instead of recording one identical failure per variant
		"benchmark run --dry-run" deinterleaved 106 GB of reads before printing the plan it was
			asked for; preparing the reads now honours the flag
		an assembler comparison could not run at all: the harness passed the cohort's reads as
			"-1 a_1.fastq,b_1.fastq", a form "metawrap2 assembly" does not accept, so both variants
			exited in under a second with "does not exist" and the results table came out empty.
			The selected samples are now concatenated into one pair first - built once and shared by
			every variant, which is also how "metawrap2 run" co-assembles a study
		"metawrap2 install-env <name>" raised a KeyError traceback for exactly the opt-in env
			names the docs tell people to install (bin_refinement-gunc, bin_refinement-checkm2,
			benchmark-amber), because an opt-in env is named after its yaml and that is not a key
			of MODULE_ENVS
		"install-env --force" never rebuilt anything. Two independent reasons: --force is also a
			global flag, so the CLI stripped it before the installer's parser saw it and the
			installer reported "already exists; skipping (use --force to recreate)" to someone who
			had just typed it; and "mamba env create --force" does not exist in mamba 2.x, where the
			command is micromamba's and rejects the flag outright. Recreating now removes the env
			first, and the global spelling reaches the installer
		a tool running inside a conda env could import packages from the user's own ~/.local
			instead of the ones its env pins, because user site-packages come first on sys.path.
			AMBER died on it - its env pins matplotlib below 3.9 for an API it calls, and a
			matplotlib 3.11 in ~/.local was imported instead. Env-wrapped commands now run with
			PYTHONNOUSERSITE=1; commands run without an env are left alone, since a tool the user
			pip-installed there is then the tool they meant to run
		one zero-length record in the assembly ended a binning run hours in, naming neither the file
			nor the record: bwa indexes it as "@SQ ... LN:0" and samtools then refuses the entire
			alignment. CAMI2's gold-standard assembly for the gastrointestinal challenge ships two, so
			every variant of the binner comparison died at the first sort. Records with no sequence are
			now left out of the working copy of the assembly, and how many were left out is said
		a comparison could not be re-run after a failure. Each variant is its own process, so
			"metawrap2 --force benchmark run" applied to the harness and to nothing it started, and every
			variant stopped at "already contains a MetaWrap2 run" - leaving hand-deleting five directories
			as the only way forward. Variants are now spawned through the CLI, the same way
			"metawrap2 run" spawns a study's steps, so --force and --resume reach them
		an interrupt arriving in the two statements between spawning a tool and waiting for it left
			that tool running with every thread it had been granted, orphaned by the run that was
			exiting - so the next thing started competed with 96 threads of a run nobody could see
		a run started in the background could not be interrupted at all. A shell sets a background
			job's SIGINT to SIG_IGN and Python leaves an inherited SIG_IGN in place, so "kill -INT" on a
			run launched with "&" or nohup - which is how a long run is launched - did nothing, silently.
			SIGINT is taken over when, and only when, it was inherited ignored
		every quant_bins abundance table with more than one bin held wrong numbers. The per-sample
			accumulators were reset through a bin name left over from the inner loop, so exactly one
			bin - whichever contig came last in the counts file - was ever cleared, and it was cleared
			partway through the pass that reads it. Every other bin therefore kept accumulating, making
			sample N's cell the median over samples 1..N, and the one bin that was cleared reported NaN
			in every sample. On two bins and two samples with the reads built so that each bin belongs
			entirely to one of them, the table came out as "bin_1 1000000 500000" / "bin_2 nan nan"
			where the right answer is "bin_1 1000000 0" / "bin_2 0 1000000". The weighted list is now
			built fresh per sample. A bin of only sub-kilobase contigs still reports NaN - that part
			was always intended, and is now the only thing NaN means
		a bin folder that had been through another module could not be quantified: the salmon path
			walked it with a bare listdir and handed every entry to the FASTA reader, so a checkm/ or
			gunc/ subdirectory raised IsADirectoryError naming only the path, and a .stats.tsv table
			was read as an empty bin. Only the FASTA files are read now
		gzipped bins were rejected before either quantifier started, although both read compression
			without noticing it - the directory check matched .fa/.fasta/.fna/.fas literally while the
			sequence readers go through smart_open and CoverM reads a gzipped genome FASTA directly.
			quant_bins accepts .gz bins; the modules that hand a bin straight to prokka or CheckM1 do
			not, since there the failure would only move into the middle of the run
		the two quantifiers could label the same bin two different ways, leaving tables that cannot be
			joined or compared. The salmon path stripped the last dotted component, which leaves
			"bin_001.fasta" for bin_001.fasta.gz where CoverM says "bin_001"; a bin file with no
			extension at all became an empty row key, and two of them collided into one row. Both
			paths now label rows with one shared function
		"metawrap2 install-env <name>" reported "OK 0/0 tools OK" for every opt-in env after building
			it - a verification that probed nothing. doctor resolved the yaml name an opt-in env is
			named after (quant_bins-coverm) as a module it had never heard of, so it had no env to look
			in and no tools to look for. It affected amber, checkm2, gunc, gtdbtk, bakta and coverm
		"mamba is not on PATH" was printed underneath a traceback, so the one error written to
			explain a PATH problem in two sentences - with both ways out named - read as a crash
			instead. It is a user's environment, not a bug, and is now reported the way a bad config
			key already was
		the sample columns of a salmon abundance table could come out in a different order on two
			identical runs, because they were written in the order an unsorted listdir returned the
			count files. Labels always travelled with their values, so no cell was ever wrong - but a
			table that changes shape between runs is not one anybody can diff

MetaWrap2 v=2.0.1
	Phase 1 foundation work: a pipeline driver, verified resume, a durable run history, one
		naming convention for bins, and a one-command install. No scientific behaviour
		changed - that is the point of this release.
	New - one command for a whole study
		"metawrap2 run <sheet|directory> -o study/" runs the pipeline end to end from a small
			TOML (or YAML) sample sheet. It owns the intermediate layout and the plumbing
			between steps that the tutorial previously asked users to do by hand with
			mkdir/mv/cat - collecting each sample's QC'ed reads into CLEAN_READS,
			concatenating them for a co-assembly, handing each module the right bin
			directory - and none of that was resumable or recorded before
		-j runs independent samples concurrently, dividing the -t thread budget between them
		--resume skips steps that already finished (state in <output>/.metawrap2/run_steps),
			--dry-run prints every module command it would run, --steps/--skip adjust the plan,
			--no-coassembly assembles per sample, and a directory can be passed instead of a
			sheet to pair up the reads in it automatically
		every step is an ordinary "metawrap2 <module>" invocation logged to study/LOGS/, so
			the driver adds no behaviour of its own and anything it does can be done by hand
		"metawrap2 run --example" prints a documented starter sheet
	New - read layouts and file naming
		paired files are now recognised in any of the usual conventions - _1/_2, _R1/_R2,
			_R1_001/_R2_001, .1/.2 - instead of only _1.fastq/_2.fastq. Illumina output no
			longer has to be renamed before MetaWrap2 will touch it. .gz and .bz2 throughout
		read_qc supports --single-end and --interleaved. Its error message had told users to
			pass those flags since 2.0.0, but neither existed. Interleaved input is split into
			mates first and then follows the ordinary paired path, because Trim Galore has no
			interleaved mode and treating it as single-end would destroy the pairing
		io/reads.py is now the single place that decides what a read filename means, and is
			actually wired in - it shipped in 2.0.0 fully tested but imported by nothing
	Fixed
		"metawrap2 run ... --dry-run" executed the run instead of planning it: the top-level
			CLI strips the global flags out of argv before a subcommand parses them, so the
			driver never saw its own --dry-run
		the driver passed a fixed -A/-B/-C to bin_refinement, so a study that selected only
			some binners failed with "INITIAL_BINNING/maxbin2_bins is not a valid directory";
			it now passes only the bin sets binning was asked to produce
		quant_bins deleted salmon's per-sample alignment directory only when there was more
			than one sample, so single-sample runs silently kept it
		bin_refinement rewrote every bin file on every invocation while normalising contig
			headers; it now only rewrites the files that actually change
		add_bins_to_blobplot keyed contigs on the whole FASTA header, so bins carrying
			metaBAT2's header annotations matched nothing and the blobplot came out entirely
			unbinned. Same contig-identity fix as the rest of 2.0.1
		a duplicated test function name meant one test had been silently shadowed and never run
	Changed - readability and lint
		choose_best_bin.py, filter_reads_for_bin_reassembly.py and
			filter_nanopore_reads_for_bin_reassembly.py were rewritten out of their
			tab-indented, module-level-sys.argv Python-2 form into documented, importable,
			tested functions. Behaviour is unchanged: the read-recruitment rewrite was checked
			byte-for-byte against the original over a real bwa-mem stream (all 12 output files
			identical), and choose_best_bin picks the same winners. Both now test SAM flags
			with bitwise masks rather than indexing into bin(flag), which raised IndexError on
			small flag values (the nanopore version had a try/except IndexError for exactly this)
		ruff now reports no findings anywhere in the repository, down from 564, and CI gates
			on it instead of running it with "|| true". Style-only rules that contradict the
			project's own conventions (printf formatting, typing.List for the py3.8 target) are
			ignored with a comment saying why; vendored code is excluded; genuinely unclosed
			file handles were fixed and deliberately long-lived ones annotated
		CI also smoke-tests the CLI: every module must print its help and plan a run with no
			external tool installed
		metawrap2.toml.example is generated from the same template "config init" writes, with a
			test that they have not drifted. The example had fallen behind and listed neither
			BLASTDB_NAME, CHECKM_DB, BAKTA_DB nor GTDBTK_DATA_PATH
	New - one command to install it all
		"metawrap2 quickstart" creates every module's conda environment, then verifies the
			result. Getting started previously meant four separate commands and a reading of
			the docs before anything ran; each was simple and none of them was what the user
			came to do. -t builds several environments at once, --binning-only installs just
			the six modules genome recovery needs rather than all thirteen, and --from-lock
			builds from the committed lockfiles
		it never downloads a database that was not asked for. The databases run from 550 MB
			to ~300 GB and take hours to days, and where they go is a decision about the
			machine, so --databases small|full|none is required to fetch anything. With no
			answer given it installs the environments, prints what each module needs and how
			large it is, and - only when a terminal is attached - offers to do it, defaulting
			to no. A non-interactive run never asks and never downloads
		it composes rather than reimplements: it calls the same install-env, install-db and
			doctor code paths, and stops at the first failing step naming the exact command to
			resume from
		"metawrap2 config migrate <file>" converts an older shell-style configuration - a file
			of KEY=/path lines meant to be sourced - into the TOML that is read now. It keeps
			the database paths, drops keys that no longer apply and says why, flags paths that
			do not exist on this machine, and leaves out the variables that only located the
			old installation's own scripts. The file is scanned, never executed
		retired module and flag names now explain themselves. "metawrap2 kraken" and
			--metabat1 used to produce an argument error that said nothing about why they were
			gone; each now names what to use instead
	Changed - a guess no longer stops a run
		the pre-flight disk-space estimate warns instead of refusing. It is a chosen multiple
			of the input size rather than a measurement, and it cannot know how large scratch
			gets at its peak because scratch is deleted when a step finishes - so it can be
			wrong in both directions, and the user knows things about their filesystem that it
			does not. strict_space_check = true under [settings] restores the hard stop, which
			is what you want on shared infrastructure where one run filling the disk is
			everyone's problem. --skip-space-check still turns it off entirely
		a config's unknown or retired *database* keys are now notes rather than errors.
			[databases] is deliberately open, so a locally patched module may read a key
			nothing here knows; and a leftover key from an older setup is harmless. Refusing to
			run binning because the config still mentioned a database only a removed module
			ever read was absurd. An unknown [settings] key is still an error - that namespace
			is small and entirely ours, and a typo there silently does nothing
	Changed - things that change your output or your scripts
		bins are now named bin_001.fasta, for every binner, counted from 1 and zero-padded.
			metaWRAP named them three ways at once: metaBAT2's came out bin.1.fa, MaxBin2's
			were renumbered bin.0.fa (from 0 - an arbitrary choice of metaWRAP's own), and
			CONCOCT's carried CONCOCT's cluster ids, so the same number meant a different
			thing in each folder. Unpadded numbers also sort bin.1, bin.10, bin.2, which
			mattered because bin sets are iterated in sorted filename order and refined bins
			are numbered in that order - so with ten or more bins the numbering depended on a
			lexicographic accident. Bin CONTENTS are unchanged; the golden tests assert the
			same contigs in the same bins. Scripts globbing *.fa need *.fasta, and if you hand
			bins to CheckM or GTDB-Tk yourself the flag is now -x fasta
		.stats files are now .stats.tsv and .contigs files .contigs.tsv, so an extension says
			what a file is. Contents unchanged; scripts globbing *.stats need *.stats.tsv
		Python 3.10 is the minimum (was 3.8). 3.8 and 3.9 are both past end of life and the
			type checker cannot target them; CI runs 3.10-3.13
		--resume now re-runs anything it cannot verify. A step previously skipped on the
			strength of a marker file alone will re-run if its outputs are missing or empty or
			its inputs have changed. A resume that used to "work" may now do real work,
			because it may have been skipping a step that never produced anything
	New - --resume that verifies instead of assuming
		a run manifest (manifest.json) records each step's command, duration, outcome, and
			every declared input and output with a content fingerprint. A step is skipped only
			if it completed, its outputs are all still present and non-empty, and none of its
			inputs has changed. Marker-file resume was wrong in two directions: a step whose
			outputs were deleted was skipped anyway, and a step whose inputs had changed was
			also skipped, silently mixing results from two different inputs
		fingerprints hash the size plus the first and last megabyte, so the check costs the
			same on a 100 GB library as on a small one. --strict-fingerprints hashes whole
			files, for the case where an edit between the sampled ends matters
		"metawrap2 status <study>" reads the manifest back: per-step status, duration, and what
			--resume would do next and why - taken from the same check --resume itself makes,
			not a reimplementation of it. --step NAME shows one step's inputs, outputs, command
			and measured cost; --audit verifies every recorded output is still present and
			non-empty; --json for scripting
		at the end of every run the same audit runs automatically. Recording an output is a
			claim and something now checks it: a step that reported success but produced
			nothing, one that declared no outputs at all (--resume can never verify it, so it
			degrades to the marker-file behaviour manifests replaced), or a file no step
			claims, are all reported then rather than discovered on the next resume
	New - failing at the cause, not three modules later
		inputs and intermediates are checked for zero bytes, wrong format, truncation
			mid-record, and truncated gzip streams before a tool is handed them - and every
			problem is reported at once rather than one per re-run. An empty FASTQ used to
			produce an empty assembly, then zero bins, then a CheckM error about file
			extensions, hours and three modules from the cause
		preflight disk-space estimates refuse to start a step the filesystem plausibly cannot
			hold (metaSPAdes needs roughly 12x its input in scratch). The global
			--skip-space-check flag, or skip_space_check = true, overrides
		an unknown [settings] key or top-level table in the config is now an error with a
			did-you-mean, rather than being silently ignored - "use_conda_env" used to look
			like it worked and do nothing. [databases] stays open to unrecognised keys, since a
			locally patched module may use its own, but a name that is nearly a known one is
			reported: CHECKM_DBB means CheckM silently gets no database
	New - surviving what actually goes wrong
		SIGTERM and SIGHUP are handled like Ctrl-C. A scheduler killing the job on a wall-clock
			or memory limit, or a dropped SSH session, used to kill the process outright,
			leaving the step recorded as "running" forever with nothing to explain it. Those
			signals now raise a KeyboardInterrupt subclass, so every finally already written
			runs, the outcome is recorded, and the exit code is 128 + the signal number
		one writer per output directory: a lock file created with O_EXCL means a second run
			writing the same -o fails immediately, naming who holds it, instead of interleaving
			with the first and leaving a plausible-looking directory whose contents came from
			two runs with different parameters. A lock whose process is gone on this host is
			taken over silently; one from another host cannot be checked, so it is believed,
			and --force overrides
		Ctrl-C marks the running step interrupted, so --resume redoes it rather than trusting
			half-written outputs
		the memory budget is divided across parallel workers, so "-m 64 -j 4" gives each worker
			16 GB rather than four workers each believing they own 64
		one scratch policy: [settings] scratch_dir, or $METAWRAP2_SCRATCH, decides temporary
			space for every module instead of each tool's own default scattering files. Unset,
			scratch stays inside the run's output directory - the one location the user chose
		the run log rotates at 256 MB, keeping 3 backups. It holds every line of every tool,
			and one pathological tool could otherwise fill the filesystem the run needs
	New - knowing and reproducing what you ran
		"metawrap2 history" is an append-only record of every run ever started on this machine,
			at ~/.metawrap2/history.jsonl. It lives outside any output directory, survives
			deleting one, and is never pruned. --module/--output/--since/--failed/--commands/
			--json/--stats filter and reshape it, and --diff RUN RUN compares two runs field by
			field: version, conda env, databases, command line. A history write can never fail
			a run
		conda lockfiles are committed for every shipped environment. "install-env --lock" writes
			envs/locks/<module>.<platform>.lock from "conda list --explicit --md5";
			--from-lock, and "doctor --fix --from-lock", restore from it. Plain
			"conda create --file" works too, so no extra tooling is needed
		run_environment.json records the environment's full conda package list, written before
			any tool runs - so a run that dies in its first command still says what it was
			running with
		the MetaWrap2 git commit is recorded alongside the version string, with the branch,
			git describe, and whether the working tree was dirty. Between releases "2.0.1"
			names many different trees, including one with local edits - the normal state of a
			package whose design invites editing the module files
		databases are identified, not just located: which of each database's defining files are
			present, their total size, and a content fingerprint from name/size/mtime. A
			database updated in place is now visible as a change rather than an
			identical-looking path, so "which analyses used the nt I just replaced?" has an
			answer
		each step's real cost is measured - peak RSS, CPU time, wall time - by reaping the child
			with os.wait4 rather than subprocess.call. getrusage(RUSAGE_CHILDREN) cannot do
			this: it reports a running maximum across every child ever waited for. "1.0 cores
			busy while given 24 threads" is visible in the log
		"metawrap2 calibrate <study>..." compares the built-in disk and memory estimates against
			real runs. Output-to-input ratio is a measurable lower bound on the disk multiplier
			(scratch is deleted when a step finishes, so no manifest can record its peak);
			peak memory is measured exactly. The report says which is which, because a
			confidently wrong disk estimate is how a run fills a filesystem four hours in
	New - bin_refinement takes any number of bin sets
		up to six bin sets, not exactly two or three, with --refine-combinations
			pairs-and-all|pairs|all choosing which subsets are refined. Two- and three-set runs
			produce byte-identical output and an identical Binning_refiner command line to
			before, which the golden tests enforce
	New - metawrap2 doctor
		"metawrap2 doctor" is the same command as "metawrap2 test" under the name people reach
			for when something is broken - and the name its own repair flag already used.
			--fix recreates exactly the environments that are missing or broken, leaves healthy
			ones alone, then re-checks and reports anything still wrong
		doctor also checks the databases each module needs, using the same definitions as the
			provenance fingerprints. A healthy environment with a half-downloaded database is
			not a working installation, and checking envs alone answered only half of "can I
			run this?"
	Fixed - wrong or missing records
		every module recorded "completed" in its history regardless of outcome. finish_run is
			called from a finally, where success and failure are indistinguishable from the
			arguments, and it defaulted to the happy path - so a run that failed, or was
			killed, or called error(), still logged success and told you nothing. The status is
			now derived from whether an exception is propagating and which one
		metaBAT2's bins were never renumbered, so its output kept the tool's own bin.1.fa naming
			while MaxBin2's and CONCOCT's were rewritten. Renumbering now happens after
			metaBAT2's non-genome outputs (bin.unbinned.fa, bin.lowDepth.fa, bin.tooShort.fa)
			are moved aside, so a rejected-contigs file can never consume a bin number, and
			bins are ordered by the number metaBAT2 gave them numerically rather than
			lexicographically
		"metawrap2 doctor" was unreachable under that name despite its own repair flag being
			--fix; only "metawrap2 test" resolved
		a dead branch in check_fasta's truncation test: "A or B and C" parses as "A or (B and
			C)", making the single-line case unreachable. The remaining condition covered it
		bin_refinement left bins* directories in the output root for more than three input sets,
			and the binning/reassembly plots crashed or reused colours with many sets
		dereplication crashed with KeyError on a bin with no row in the stats file - which
			happens when a run is interrupted between writing bins and re-scoring them. Such a
			bin is now treated as the worst possible bin, with a warning, rather than aborting
			the refinement
		--subsample in kraken2 needed many times the file size in RAM, making it unusable on
			exactly the large libraries it exists to shrink. Reservoir sampling now bounds peak
			memory by the requested depth
	Changed - logging and typing
		logging is a real logging.Logger throughout, with a banner formatter on the console and
			a plain one in the file. Every run writes <output>/metawrap2.log; wrapped tools'
			stdout and stderr are funnelled into it at DEBUG, tagged [stdout]/[stderr], while
			still streaming to the screen. The console handler resolves sys.stdout at emit
			time, which fixes silent or erroring log output when the stream is swapped
		repetitive tool output is collapsed and progress-bar spam filtered from the captured
			logs - measured at 1.8 MB down to 30 KB on a real three-sample run.
			--verbose-logs restores the raw stream
		tqdm progress bars wherever MetaWrap2 itself is doing the work - splitting bins,
			subsampling reads, scanning FASTA - not only where a wrapped tool prints something
		type annotations throughout, checked by mypy in CI and in the pre-push hook
		the package is pip-installable as an ordinary wheel. The environment yamls and
			lockfiles moved under src/metawrap2/envs/ and ship as package data, so they
			resolve from an installed wheel and not only from a source checkout
		the README now describes MetaWrap2 rather than mirroring the metaWRAP 1 README
	Changed - the test suite gates a push
		456 tests, up from 245. New suites cover the manifest and its audit, input validation,
			the run history, the scratch policy, the lockfiles, "doctor --fix", the run lock,
			signal handling, "status", and bin naming
		15 golden tests pin bin_refinement's exact output - real Binning_refiner, real
			consolidation and dereplication, with only CheckM replaced by a deterministic
			stand-in - so the N-set generalisation could be proved not to change the 2- and
			3-set results
		a golden end-to-end test runs binning into bin_refinement, quant_bins and annotate_bins
			against stub tools on PATH, pinning the output tree and the .stats.tsv contract
			byte-for-byte. It catches what unit tests structurally cannot: a module writing
			where the next one does not look
		black, isort, ruff, mypy and pytest must all pass before a push (pre-commit pre-push
			hooks) and all run in CI on Python 3.10-3.13. Formatting is checked, never
			applied, in CI: a job that rewrites your code and then passes teaches you nothing
		ruff-format was removed from pre-commit - two formatters fight each other
		a weekly Lockfiles workflow creates conda environments from the committed lockfiles -
			the only check that they do what they claim - and opens a PR when a re-solve makes
			them drift, so drift arrives as a reviewable diff rather than a surprise months
			later. It also runs on any PR touching src/metawrap2/envs/

MetaWrap2 v=2.0.1
	Bug fixes and install/usability work from a full end-to-end validation run on real data
	(3 metaHIT gut metagenomes, every module, all databases).
	Fixed - would break any real run
		MetaWrap2's own helper scripts (metawrap2.scripts.*, metawrap2.vendor.*) were invoked
			as "python -m ..." *inside* a module's conda env, which contains only that
			module's external tools - no python, no metawrap2, no matplotlib. Every such call
			failed. They now run in the host interpreter by absolute path (metawrap2.pyrun);
			affected bin_refinement, reassemble_bins, quant_bins, and blobology
		"mamba run --no-capture-output" is a conda-only flag; mamba 2.x mis-parses it and
			every wrapped command died with "exec: --: invalid option", which looked like the
			tool was missing. The flag is now used only when "run --help" advertises it
		"metawrap2 test" probed tools with "bash -lc", and a login shell rebuilds PATH from
			the user's profile, discarding the env's own bin directory - so every tool in
			every healthy env was reported MISSING. Now uses "bash -c"
		numpy/pandas/matplotlib/seaborn were undeclared dependencies even though the bundled
			plotting helpers import them; added (plus tqdm for progress bars)
		envs/blobology.yaml pip-installed "metawrap2" from PyPI, where it does not exist, so
			the env could never be created. The blobology env now holds only external tools
		envs/classify_bins.yaml was missing blast, though the module shells out to blastn
		envs/assembly.yaml did not pin python, so QUAST landed on python>=3.12 and died with
			"No module named 'distutils'"; pinned python<3.12
		envs/bin_refinement.yaml did not pin setuptools, so CheckM1 died with "No module
			named 'pkg_resources'" on setuptools>=81; pinned setuptools<81
	Fixed - wrong results from contig-identity mismatches
		contig identity is now consistently "the header up to the first whitespace"
			(metawrap2.io.seqio.contig_id) everywhere contigs are compared between files.
			Current metaBAT2 (2.18) appends " total_depth=.. sample_depths=.." to every
			contig header; MetaWrap2 keyed on the *whole* header in some places and on the
			first token in others, so metaBAT2 bins matched nothing:
			- bin_refinement measured 0% overlap between a metaBAT2 bin and the same bin
			  from another binner (the threshold is 80%), so consolidation silently never
			  combined metaBAT2 with MaxBin2/CONCOCT - the module appeared to work and
			  quietly produced a worse bin set
			- quant_bins aborted outright with "None of the contigs/scaffolds in the -a
			  metagenomic assembly file were present in the bin files"
			metaBAT2's bins are also normalised at the source now, so MetaWrap2's own
			outputs carry clean headers
	Fixed - data loss and wrong results
		read_qc with --skip-trimming --skip-bmtagger moved the user's *raw input* FASTQ into
			the output directory (os.replace on a path outside the output dir). It now copies
			when the source is not an intermediate it created
		read_qc left the decompressed copies of gzipped inputs in the output directory
			forever - several GB per sample of pure duplication. They are now removed once
			the final reads are written
		binning split CONCOCT clusters by truncating each contig name at the *first* ".",
			so any assembler whose names contain a dot - metaSPAdes emits
			"NODE_1_length_5000_cov_3.5" - had unrelated contigs collapsed onto one key and
			scattered into the wrong bins. Names are now used verbatim. Splitting is also
			idempotent now (it appended, so a re-run duplicated every sequence)
		metaBAT2's --unbinned output (bin.unbinned.fa) sat in the bin directory where
			bin_refinement and CheckM scored it as if it were a genome; it is moved aside
		rm_short_contigs parsed the contig length out of a SPAdes-style header and stopped at
			the first short contig; it now measures the sequence, so it works on any
			assembler and does not depend on the file being sorted longest-first
		refinement.dereplicate raised KeyError and aborted the whole run if a bin file had no
			row in the .stats file; such bins now lose contested contigs and a warning is printed
	Fixed - --dry-run
		--dry-run only ever printed the first one or two commands of a module: the
			orchestration then tried to consume outputs that a dry run never produced and
			died with a raw traceback, or with a misleading "Something went wrong with X".
			Every module now prints its complete pipeline (dry-run-aware filesystem helpers
			in modules/_common.py), writes no result data, and still records the plan in
			run_commands.txt. Covered by a test per module
	Fixed - log noise
		run.stdout/run.stderr are now filtered for runaway repetition and progress-bar
			redraws. Measured on a 3-sample run, INITIAL_BINNING/run.stderr was 1.8 MB, of
			which 23,770 of 24,770 lines were two metaBAT2 warnings repeated ~11,900 times
			each; filtering takes it to 30 KB (98.5% smaller). Repeats are counted, not
			discarded: the first 20 are kept verbatim and a summary line reports how many
			more there were. A command whose stdout *is* its result is never filtered, the
			error message in a ToolError still shows the tool's real last output, and
			--verbose-logs restores the raw behaviour
		a failed per-bin SPAdes reassembly now reports the ERROR lines from that bin's
			spades.log instead of guessing "It is possible there were not enough reads",
			and the all-bins-failed message lists the likely shared causes
	Fixed - scalability and robustness
		"kraken2 --subsample" materialised every read of both files in memory before
			sampling, so it died on exactly the large libraries it exists to shrink; it now
			uses reservoir sampling, bounded by the subsample size
		the BLAST database no longer has to be named "nt" with 2-digit volume numbering:
			BLASTDB_NAME selects the database, and presence is detected across single-volume,
			2-digit, 3-digit, and alias (.nal) layouts (BLAST v5 uses 3 digits)
		conda_env_exists guessed at a couple of hard-coded prefixes; it now asks conda/mamba
			for the real environment directories
		"metawrap2 install-env" located envs/ by a fixed relative path that only works in a
			source checkout; it now searches upward and honours $METAWRAP2_ENVS_DIR
		fix_megahit_contig_naming read len=/multi= by position, which breaks across MEGAHIT
			releases; it now reads them by name and falls back to the measured length
		a bin_refinement run whose consolidation yields no bins now explains that nothing
			met the -c/-x thresholds and points at the .stats files, instead of failing
			inside CheckM with "No bins found. Check the extension (-x) used to identify bins"
		every module now makes its path arguments absolute up front (modules._common.
			absolutize_paths). Tools that must run with cwd=<output dir> because they write
			into the working directory - taxator-tk's binner, the plotting helpers - were
			being handed paths relative to the *original* directory, producing
			"cd out && ... out/file" (i.e. out/out/file). So classify_bins and the final
			bin_refinement plot both failed for anyone who passed a relative -o, which is
			what the tutorial itself does
		binner and taxknife now get TAXATORTK_TAXONOMY_NCBI, which only taxator had. binner
			aborted with "Specify the folder containing the NCBI taxonomy dump files as
			TAXATORTK_TAXONOMY_NCBI environment variable" - after the slow megablast step
			had already completed
		kraken2 looked for names.dmp/nodes.dmp only under <db>/taxonomy/, which is the
			kraken2-build layout; every *prebuilt* database keeps them at the top level, so
			the module crashed with a bare FileNotFoundError after the slow classification
			had already finished. It now searches both and falls back to TAXDUMP
		"superkingdom" is no longer assumed: NCBI renamed that rank to "domain" and a
			current taxdump has zero superkingdom nodes, so blobology's superkingdom
			blobplot said "Not annotated" for every contig - losing the
			Bacteria/Archaea/Eukaryota split the plot exists for - and prune_blast_hits
			discarded domain-ranked hits. Both ranks are now accepted as synonyms
		metaBAT2's non-genome outputs (bin.lowDepth.fa, bin.tooShort.fa, bin.unbinned.fa
			and its two .txt summaries) are moved out of the bin directory into
			metabat2_extra/ so CheckM and bin_refinement do not score them as genomes
		gc_cov_annotate shells out to samtools, which is in the blobology conda env and not
			on the host PATH where the helper now runs; the caller passes its absolute path
		seaborn's clustermap needs scipy, which was not declared - the quant_bins heatmap
			failed at the very last step of the module
		annotate_bins iterated every file in the bin folder, including .stats/.contigs
			sidecars; it now only annotates fasta files, and says so if there are none
		sam_to_fastq wrote a ">" header into a 4-line FASTQ record, making the file neither
			valid FASTA nor valid FASTQ; it now writes "@" (verified to be equivalent for
			MEGAHIT, the only in-pipeline consumer)
	New - opt-in modern tools, now actually wired up
		"bin_refinement --checkm2" scores bins with CheckM2 instead of CheckM1: fast, no
			pplacer, none of CheckM1's ~40GB-per-thread memory wall. Produces the same
			.stats contract, so every later step works unchanged
		"classify_bins --gtdbtk" classifies against GTDB with GTDB-Tk in place of the
			MEGABLAST -> taxator-tk -> binner -> taxknife chain, writing the same
			bin_taxonomy.tab
		"annotate_bins --bakta" annotates with Bakta instead of the unmaintained PROKKA,
			with the same output layout (--bakta-db or BAKTA_DB)
		these three environments shipped in 2.0.0 with no flags to select them, and the docs
			described flags that did not exist
	New - installation and usability
		"metawrap2 install-db" downloads, unpacks, indexes, and *configures* every database,
			including the "checkm data setRoot" step that is easy to forget and otherwise
			surfaces much later as a confusing bin_refinement failure. "--list" shows sizes
			and which module needs what; "--small" installs capped-but-real builds for
			testing; already-installed databases are skipped, so it is safe to re-run
		"install-env -t N" and "install-db -t N" do the work N at a time (env solves and
			database downloads are independent and network-bound, so this is close to a
			linear speedup). Per-env solver output goes to its own log file instead of
			interleaving on screen
		"-t/--threads" now defaults to [settings] threads from metawrap2.toml instead of a
			silent 1 - which on a metagenome was the difference between hours and days - and
			accepts "-t all". Out-of-range values are clamped with a warning
		a module whose conda env is missing now fails immediately with the exact
			"metawrap2 install-env <module>" command to fix it, instead of a mamba error
			about a missing prefix part-way into a long run
		progress bars (tqdm) on the long loops that used to look like a hang: read
			recruitment, per-bin reassembly and annotation, CheckM over bin sets,
			per-sample quantification and mapping, CONCOCT bin splitting, subsampling.
			They go to stderr and switch off when stderr is not a terminal, so they never
			corrupt a redirected data file or flood run.stderr
		"metawrap2 check" now exits non-zero when a selected module is missing a database it
			cannot run without (it only ever checked conda envs, despite saying otherwise),
			and no longer reports setting-valued keys like BLASTDB_NAME as a missing path
		trim_galore now gets -j (capped at 4, where cutadapt stops scaling) instead of
			running single-threaded regardless of -t
	Testing
		added 50+ regression tests pinning every fix above, and made two environment-
			dependent tests hermetic (one passed only before the conda envs existed)

MetaWrap2 v=2.0.0
	MetaWrap2 is the Python-native successor to metaWRAP. Same algorithms, same external
	tools, same results as metaWRAP 1.x - but the pipeline is now entirely Python (no Bash,
	Perl, or R), with modern packaging and per-module conda environments.
	General
		command is now "metawrap2" (Python package "metawrap2"); modules are unchanged
		every module is now a Python module: `metawrap2 <module>` or `python -m metawrap2.modules.<module>`
		each module runs in its own conda environment "metawrap2-<module>" holding only that
			module's tools, so environments resolve quickly and independently
		commands each module runs are visible, editable templates at the top of each module file;
			any surfaced constant can also be overridden via ~/.metawrap2/config.toml
		configuration moved from the sourced config-metawrap file to metawrap2.toml
		removed all Bash, Perl, and R; blobology and Binning_refiner steps now run in Python
		"metawrap2 check" verifies tools, conda envs, and databases before a run;
			"metawrap2 install-env" creates (and verifies) a module's conda environment;
			"metawrap2 test" probes each module env + key software, runs unit tests, and
			prints an installed/missing/broken status map
		each run writes provenance into its output dir: run_config.json (full config +
			defaults + timing + tool versions used), run_commands.txt (exact commands,
			re-runnable), provenance.txt (full lineage, propagated from any MetaWrap2
			inputs used), and run.stdout/run.stderr (full tool output, also shown live)
		all external commands run through a single command handler that wraps them in the
			module's conda env, streams output to screen and to run.stdout/run.stderr, records
			them for provenance, and cleans up child processes on Ctrl-C
		conda environments are created and driven with mamba (faster); install-env
			bootstraps mamba if needed. Set use_conda_envs=false to run tools off PATH
		global options: "--dry-run" (record/print commands without running), "--force"
			(overwrite an existing output dir; otherwise MetaWrap2 refuses to clobber a run),
			and "--resume" (reuse an output dir and skip stages that already finished, tracked
			under <outdir>/.metawrap2/steps)
		"metawrap2 config init|show" scaffolds and inspects metawrap2.toml;
			"metawrap2 completion bash|zsh" prints a shell-completion script
		"metawrap2 test" now reports the version of each working tool (--verbose lists all)
		warns when input/output paths contain spaces or shell metacharacters
		project tooling: CI tests across Python 3.8-3.12 with coverage, a pre-commit config,
			CONTRIBUTING guide, issue/PR templates, and an mkdocs documentation site
		flowchart figures are regenerated in Python from their draw.io sources
			(flowcharts/make_flowcharts.py); no Perl/R/draw.io needed
		dropped the deprecated kraken1 module (use kraken2) and metaBAT1 binner (use metaBAT2)
	Coming from metaWRAP 1.x
		replace "metaWRAP" with "metawrap2" in your commands; module names and outputs are the same
		see README "Coming from metaWRAP 1.x" for the full flag/output mapping

metaWRAP v=1.5 (superseded by MetaWrap2 2.0.0)
	General
		Python scripts now run under Python 3 (Python 2 reached end of life)
		sequence inputs can now be gzip- or bzip2-compressed (.gz/.bz2)
		added "check" command to verify tools and databases are available before a run
		dependencies loosened from old fixed versions and grouped by module under envs/
		clearer, tool-specific error messages when an external program fails
		reset the example database paths in config-metawrap to generic placeholders
	Read_QC
		gzipped read files (.gz) are now accepted as input
	Bin_refinement
		use a portable method to measure bin file sizes (works on macOS/BSD)
		bin consolidation is now deterministic regardless of input ordering
	Blobology
		bowtie2-build now uses the requested number of threads
		replaced the Perl and R helper scripts with Python; the contig annotation
			table and blobplot images are now generated with Python (no Perl/R needed)
	Reassemble_bins
		reassembly now uses one thread per bin as documented, reducing oversubscription

